PU accesses directly","open image","secondary storage"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Boot program","windows":{"definition":[["The start-up instructions stored in ",{"answer":"ROM"}," that run when the computer is switched on."]],"characteristics":[["Runs automatically at power on, then loads the ",{"answer":"operating system"}," from secondary storage into RAM. Kept in ROM so it survives power off."]],"examples":[["Press power: the boot program runs first and checks the ",{"answer":"hardware"},", then Windows loads."]],"nonExamples":[["The boot program is not the operating system and is not stored in RAM: RAM is ",{"answer":"empty"}," at power on because it is volatile."]]},"wordBank":["empty","hardware","operating system","ROM"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.2/frayer-2.pdf","sourceSha256":"dbe945ccd44c26ebc04e6dd9d497ff7c73afb7ef6119663d2f8eba29f978ab09","code":"1.2.2","title":"Secondary storage","terms":[{"term":"Secondary storage","windows":{"definition":[[{"answer":"Non-volatile"}," storage used to keep files, data and software long term, even when the power is off."]],"characteristics":[["Three types: magnetic, ",{"answer":"optical"}," and solid state. Larger capacity than RAM but slower, and not accessed directly by the CPU. Needed because RAM is volatile."]],"examples":[["A smart TV keeps its apps and recorded shows on an SSD, so they remain after it is ",{"answer":"switched off"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["RAM and ROM are primary storage, not secondary. Secondary storage is not just for ",{"answer":"backups"},": it holds the operating system and all installed software."]]},"wordBank":["backups","Non-volatile","optical","switched off"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Solid-state storage","windows":{"definition":[["Storage that holds data electronically in ",{"answer":"flash memory"}," chips; examples are SSDs, USB memory sticks and SD cards."]],"characteristics":[["Fast; durable as it has ",{"answer":"no moving parts"},"; small, light, low power. Costs more per GB than magnetic and has a limited number of write cycles."]],"examples":[["A student carries a presentation between school and home on a USB stick: small, robust and ",{"answer":"portable"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Not a hard disk drive: an HDD has spinning platters and is ",{"answer":"magnetic"},". Solid state does not last for ever: its cells wear out after very many writes."]]},"wordBank":["flash memory","magnetic","no moving parts","portable"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Magnetic storage","windows":{"definition":[["Storage that records data by ",{"answer":"magnetising"}," tiny areas of a spinning disk (platter) or tape to represent 1s and 0s."]],"characteristics":[["Very high capacity for its price. Has ",{"answer":"moving parts"}," (platters, read/write head), so it is slower than solid state and damaged if dropped or knocked."]],"examples":[["A musician's PC stores thousands of recordings on a 4 TB hard disk drive, which has a low ",{"answer":"cost per GB"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Not suited to a phone or camera: its moving parts break when knocked. A DVD is ",{"answer":"optical"}," and a USB stick is solid state; neither is magnetic."]]},"wordBank":["cost per GB","magnetising","moving parts","optical"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Optical storage","windows":{"definition":[["Storage that uses a ",{"answer":"laser"}," to read and write data as pits and lands on the surface of a spinning disc."]],"characteristics":[["CD, DVD and Blu-ray. Low cost per disc, thin and light, but ",{"answer":"low capacity"},", slow access and easily scratched. Needs an optical drive to read it."]],"examples":[["A school hands out a film of the school play on DVD, as discs are very ",{"answer":"cheap"}," to copy and post."]],"nonExamples":[["A poor choice for a computer's main drive: a Blu-ray holds only about 25 GB. A USB stick is ",{"answer":"solid state"},", as it has no disc."]]},"wordBank":["cheap","laser","low capacity","solid state"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Capacity","windows":{"definition":[["The ",{"answer":"amount of data"}," that a storage device can hold, measured in units such as GB or TB."]],"characteristics":[["Optical is lowest (CD about 700 MB, DVD 4.7 GB, Blu-ray 25 GB). Solid state: up to a few TB. ",{"answer":"Magnetic"},": most for the money (HDD roughly 1 TB to 20 TB)."]],"examples":[["1000 recordings of 3 MB each need 3000 MB = ",{"answer":"3 GB"},", so a 4.7 GB DVD would be big enough."]],"nonExamples":[["Capacity is not speed. Do not always choose the biggest device: say the capacity is ",{"answer":"sufficient"}," for the files in the question. 200 000 KB is only 200 MB."]]},"wordBank":["3 GB","amount of data","Magnetic","sufficient"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Speed","windows":{"definition":[["How quickly data can be ",{"answer":"read from or written to"}," the device (its access and data transfer speed)."]],"characteristics":[["Solid state is ",{"answer":"fastest"},", as nothing has to move. Magnetic is slower: the platter must spin and the head must move. Optical is the slowest."]],"examples":[["A video editor picks an SSD so that huge files ",{"answer":"load and save"}," quickly; an HDD would mean waiting."]],"nonExamples":[["Speed does not always matter: if files are only being copied to take home, fast access is ",{"answer":"not required"},", so a cheaper device is suitable."]]},"wordBank":["fastest","load and save","not required","read from or written to"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Portability","windows":{"definition":[["How easy the device is to ",{"answer":"carry around"},", which depends on its physical size and weight and on the power it needs."]],"characteristics":[["Solid state: most portable (tiny, light, low power). Optical: thin, light discs, but they need a ",{"answer":"drive"}," to read them. Magnetic: external HDDs are bulkier."]],"examples":[["Nina moves photos from her camera to her laptop on a tiny SD card that ",{"answer":"fits in a pocket"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["An internal drive in a desktop PC or smart TV never moves, so portability is ",{"answer":"not needed"},": do not use it to justify that choice."]]},"wordBank":["carry around","drive","fits in a pocket","not needed"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Durability","windows":{"definition":[["How well the device ",{"answer":"resists physical damage"},", such as being dropped, knocked, scratched or heated."]],"characteristics":[["Solid state: most durable (robust), as no moving parts. Magnetic: a knock can crash the ",{"answer":"read/write head"},". Optical: easily scratched; harmed by heat and sunlight."]],"examples":[["A dashcam is ",{"answer":"shaken"}," all day while driving, so it records to a solid state memory card, not an HDD."]],"nonExamples":[["Durable does not mean it lasts for ever: that is closer to ",{"answer":"reliability"},". A magnetic HDD is durable enough if the computer is never moved."]]},"wordBank":["read/write head","reliability","resists physical damage","shaken"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":26,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":27,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Reliability","windows":{"definition":[["How ",{"answer":"consistently"}," the device works over time without failing, losing data or corrupting data."]],"characteristics":[["Solid state: very reliable, but has limited ",{"answer":"write cycles"},". Magnetic: reliable if kept still, but moving parts wear out. Optical: discs degrade and scratch."]],"examples":[["A company stores backups on magnetic hard drives ",{"answer":"kept still"}," in a server room, so they work for years."]],"nonExamples":[["Reliable is not the same as durable: durability is surviving ",{"answer":"drops and knocks"},"; reliability is lasting through years of reading and writing."]]},"wordBank":["consistently","drops and knocks","kept still","write cycles"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":29,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":30,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Cost","windows":{"definition":[["The price of the device compared with how much it holds, usually judged as the ",{"answer":"cost per GB"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Magnetic: lowest cost per GB for large capacity. Optical: very cheap per disc, but low capacity. ",{"answer":"Solid state"},": highest cost per GB, though prices are falling."]],"examples":[["A 2 TB HDD at £50 costs 2.5p per GB; a 2 TB SSD at £120 costs ",{"answer":"6p"}," per GB."]],"nonExamples":[["Cheapest to buy is not cheapest per GB: a £5, 16 GB USB stick costs about ",{"answer":"31p"}," per GB. Use cost to justify a choice when the budget or a large capacity matters."]]},"wordBank":["31p","6p","cost per GB","Solid state"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":32,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":33,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.2/frayer-3.pdf","sourceSha256":"db36713263af8c1593c4d8acca61708ea2402a9dab945bb6923bfacc28724f8f","code":"1.2.3","title":"Units of data storage","terms":[{"term":"Byte","windows":{"definition":[["A byte is a group of ",{"answer":"8 bits"},"; it is the unit normally used to measure file sizes and storage capacity."]],"characteristics":[["1 byte = 2 nibbles. One unsigned byte has ",{"answer":"256"}," different patterns, holding the values 0 to 255. One ASCII character is stored in 1 byte."]],"examples":[["A 40-character ASCII text file needs 40 bytes, which is 40 x 8 = ",{"answer":"320 bits"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["8 bytes is not the same as 8 bits: 8 bytes = 64 bits. A byte is also not the smallest unit of data; that is the ",{"answer":"bit"},"."]]},"wordBank":["256","320 bits","8 bits","bit"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Bit","windows":{"definition":[["A bit is a single ",{"answer":"binary digit"},", either 0 or 1; it is the smallest unit of data in a computer."]],"characteristics":[["A bit has only ",{"answer":"two states"},", because computers are made of switches that are either on (1) or off (0). 4 bits = 1 nibble and 8 bits = 1 byte."]],"examples":[["One bit can store the state of a door sensor: 0 = closed and ",{"answer":"1"}," = open."]],"nonExamples":[["A bit cannot hold the digit 2. Denary 2 needs two bits and is written ",{"answer":"10"}," in binary. A bit is not a byte: a byte is 8 bits."]]},"wordBank":["1","10","binary digit","two states"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Nibble","windows":{"definition":[["A nibble is a group of ",{"answer":"4 bits"},", which is half a byte."]],"characteristics":[["A nibble has 16 patterns, 0000 to 1111 (denary 0 to 15), so it matches exactly one ",{"answer":"hexadecimal"}," digit. 2 nibbles = 1 byte."]],"examples":[["The nibble 1010 is 8 + 2 = denary 10, which is the single hex digit ",{"answer":"A"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["4 nibbles is not 4 bytes: 4 nibbles = 16 bits = ",{"answer":"2 bytes"},". Do not confuse a nibble (4 bits) with a byte (8 bits)."]]},"wordBank":["2 bytes","4 bits","A","hexadecimal"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Kilobyte to petabyte (KB, MB, GB, TB, PB)","windows":{"definition":[["The units used to measure file size and storage capacity, where each unit is ",{"answer":"1000"}," times larger than the one before."]],"characteristics":[["Order from smallest: bit, ",{"answer":"nibble"},", byte, kilobyte, megabyte, gigabyte, terabyte, petabyte. OCR uses 1000 as the multiplier but also accepts 1024."]],"examples":[["A photo is about 3 MB, a film about 4 GB and a hard disk about 2 TB, which is ",{"answer":"2000 GB"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A bigger number does not mean a bigger file: 2000 KB is only ",{"answer":"2 MB"},", so a 200 MB file is far larger than a 2000 KB file."]]},"wordBank":["1000","2 MB","2000 GB","nibble"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Converting between units","windows":{"definition":[["Changing a data quantity from one unit to another by multiplying or dividing by 1000 for each step, or by ",{"answer":"8"}," between bits and bytes."]],"characteristics":[["Going to a smaller unit: ",{"answer":"multiply"},", so the number gets bigger. Going to a larger unit: divide. TB to MB is two steps, so multiply by 1000 twice."]],"examples":[["2 TB = 2 x 1000 = 2000 GB, then 2000 x 1000 = ",{"answer":"2 000 000"}," MB."]],"nonExamples":[["4 500 000 bytes is not 4500 MB. Bytes to MB is two steps up (bytes, KB, MB), so divide by 1000 twice to get ",{"answer":"4.5 MB"},"."]]},"wordBank":["2 000 000","4.5 MB","8","multiply"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Binary data","windows":{"definition":[["Binary data is data held as patterns of 1s and 0s; all data needs to be converted into a binary format to be ",{"answer":"processed"}," by a computer."]],"characteristics":[["Computers are made of ",{"answer":"switches"}," (transistors) with only two states: on = 1 and off = 0. Numbers, text, images, sound and instructions are all stored as bits."]],"examples":[["The letter A is stored using its ASCII code 65, which is the bit pattern ",{"answer":"01000001"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A computer cannot store letters, colours or sound waves directly. An analogue sound wave must be sampled and converted to ",{"answer":"digital"}," data first."]]},"wordBank":["01000001","digital","processed","switches"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"File size calculation","windows":{"definition":[["Working out how much ",{"answer":"storage"}," a file needs, in bits or bytes, from the number of items of data and the number of bits used for each item."]],"characteristics":[["Image: width x height x colour depth. Sound: sample rate x duration x ",{"answer":"bit depth"},". Text: bits per character x number of characters. Divide bits by 8 for bytes."]],"examples":[["Image of 800 x 500 pixels with 8-bit colour: 800 x 500 x 8 = 3 200 000 bits = ",{"answer":"400 KB"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["800 x 500 x 8 gives bits, not bytes. Forgetting to divide by 8 gives an answer 8 times too big. ",{"answer":"Metadata"}," makes the real file slightly larger."]]},"wordBank":["400 KB","bit depth","Metadata","storage"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.2/frayer-4.pdf","sourceSha256":"444e2b669a506fb91f4dff8c5d1e5d3f80520338e62e522d0ac6cc5279685b4e","code":"1.2.4","title":"Data storage","terms":[{"term":"Binary (base 2)","windows":{"definition":[["A number system that uses only two digits, ",{"answer":"0 and 1"},"; each place value is 2 times the one to its right."]],"characteristics":[["8-bit place values are 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1. An 8-bit unsigned number holds 0 to ",{"answer":"255"},". Computers use binary because switches are either on or off."]],"examples":[["00110001 = 32 + 16 + 1 = denary ",{"answer":"49"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Binary 10 is not ten: it is (1 x 2) + 0 = denary ",{"answer":"2"},". Always read binary digits using their place values, not as a denary number."]]},"wordBank":["0 and 1","2","255","49"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Hexadecimal (base 16)","windows":{"definition":[["A number system with ",{"answer":"16"}," digits: 0 to 9, then A to F for the values 10 to 15."]],"characteristics":[["One hex digit represents one ",{"answer":"nibble"}," (4 bits), so a byte is 2 hex digits, 00 to FF. Hex is shorter and easier for people to read than binary."]],"examples":[["F4 = (15 x 16) + 4 = denary 244, which is ",{"answer":"11110100"}," in binary."]],"nonExamples":[["F is 15, not 16. Denary 16 is written ",{"answer":"10"}," in hex: one lot of 16 and no units. Computers still store binary; hex is just easier for humans."]]},"wordBank":["10","11110100","16","nibble"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Denary (base 10)","windows":{"definition":[["The everyday number system with ten digits, ",{"answer":"0 to 9"},"; each place value is 10 times the one to its right."]],"characteristics":[["Place values are 1s, 10s, 100s. Also called base 10 or ",{"answer":"decimal"},". An 8-bit binary number holds denary 0 to 255; 2 hex digits hold the same range."]],"examples":[["Denary 102 = 64 + 32 + 4 + 2, so it is ",{"answer":"01100110"}," in binary and 66 in hex."]],"nonExamples":[["Denary 31 and hex 31 are different values: hex 31 = (3 x 16) + 1 = denary ",{"answer":"49"},". Always check which base a number is written in."]]},"wordBank":["0 to 9","01100110","49","decimal"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Binary addition","windows":{"definition":[["Adding two binary numbers column by column, starting from the ",{"answer":"right"},", with a carry into the next column when a column totals 2 or more."]],"characteristics":[["Rules: 0+0 = 0, 0+1 = 1, 1+1 = 0 carry 1, 1+1+1 = ",{"answer":"1 carry 1"},". Show the carries in your working to gain the method mark."]],"examples":[["00110101 + 00011010 = ",{"answer":"01001111"},". Check in denary: 53 + 26 = 79."]],"nonExamples":[["1 + 1 is not 2 in binary, because the digit 2 does not exist. Write ",{"answer":"0"}," and carry 1, which makes binary 10."]]},"wordBank":["0","01001111","1 carry 1","right"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Binary shift","windows":{"definition":[["Moving all the bits in a binary number left or right by a set number of places, filling the empty places with ",{"answer":"zeros"},"."]],"characteristics":[["A left shift multiplies by 2 for each place; a right shift ",{"answer":"divides"}," by 2 for each place. Bits moved off the end are lost."]],"examples":[["00010100 shifted left 3 places is ",{"answer":"10100000"},": 20 x 8 = 160."]],"nonExamples":[["'Shift the bits' is not enough for 2 marks. Give the direction and the number of places, for example ",{"answer":"left"}," 3 places to multiply by 8."]]},"wordBank":["10100000","divides","left","zeros"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Overflow error","windows":{"definition":[["An error that occurs when the result of a calculation needs more ",{"answer":"bits"}," than are available to store it."]],"characteristics":[["In 8 bits the largest value is 255. A result of 256 or more needs a ",{"answer":"9th bit"},", which cannot be stored, so the answer held is incorrect."]],"examples":[["11001000 + 01100100 (200 + 100) = 1 00101100. The extra 1 is lost, leaving ",{"answer":"44"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A carry between columns is not an overflow. Overflow only happens when there is a carry out of the ",{"answer":"most significant bit"},"."]]},"wordBank":["44","9th bit","bits","most significant bit"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.2/frayer-5.pdf","sourceSha256":"ed80e52fe74424260c9bd3ca360138861528c2fd90bdcfe48653e360c4c1bacb","code":"1.2.5","title":"Data storage Numbers","terms":[{"term":"Denary to binary conversion","windows":{"definition":[["Rewriting a base 10 number as the equivalent binary number, using the ",{"answer":"place values"}," 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1."]],"characteristics":[["Work from the left: if the place value fits, write 1 and ",{"answer":"subtract"}," it; if not, write 0. Repeated division by 2 also works. Show working for the method mark."]],"examples":[["221 = 128 + 64 + 16 + 8 + 4 + 1, so 221 in binary is ",{"answer":"11011101"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["38 is not 100110 in an 8-bit answer. Add ",{"answer":"leading zeros"}," on the left to fill all 8 bits: 00100110."]]},"wordBank":["11011101","leading zeros","place values","subtract"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Binary to denary conversion","windows":{"definition":[["Rewriting a binary number in base 10 by ",{"answer":"adding"}," together the place values of every column that contains a 1."]],"characteristics":[["Write 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1 above the bits. Ignore any column holding ",{"answer":"0"},". The largest 8-bit answer is 255 (11111111)."]],"examples":[["11001011 = 128 + 64 + 8 + 2 + 1 = ",{"answer":"203"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Do not just count the 1s: 11110000 has four 1s, but its value is 128 + 64 + 32 + 16 = ",{"answer":"240"},"."]]},"wordBank":["0","203","240","adding"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Binary to hexadecimal conversion","windows":{"definition":[["Rewriting a binary number in base 16 by splitting it into ",{"answer":"nibbles"}," and replacing each one with a single hex digit."]],"characteristics":[["Split the 8 bits into two groups of 4. Convert each group to denary (8, 4, 2, 1), then write 10 to 15 as ",{"answer":"A to F"},". One byte always gives 2 hex digits."]],"examples":[["10110000 splits into 1011 and 0000. 1011 = 11 and 0000 = 0, so the hex is ",{"answer":"B0"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["1011 0000 is not hex 110: the value 11 must be written as one digit. Do not drop the ",{"answer":"zero"}," either: B0 is 176 but B alone is only 11."]]},"wordBank":["A to F","B0","nibbles","zero"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Hexadecimal to denary conversion","windows":{"definition":[["Rewriting a 2-digit hex number in base 10: multiply the first digit by 16, then ",{"answer":"add"}," the second digit."]],"characteristics":[["Hex place values are 16s and 1s. Change letters first: A = 10, up to F = 15. Or convert each digit to a 4-bit ",{"answer":"nibble"},", then convert the 8 bits to denary."]],"examples":[["2F = (2 x 16) + 15 = 32 + 15 = ",{"answer":"47"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Hex 2F is not 2 + 15 = 17, and it is not 215. Each digit must first be multiplied by its ",{"answer":"place value"},": the 2 is worth 2 x 16 = 32."]]},"wordBank":["47","add","nibble","place value"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Place value","windows":{"definition":[["The amount a digit is worth because of its ",{"answer":"position"}," in a number."]],"characteristics":[["Binary place values ",{"answer":"double"}," each step to the left: 1, 2, 4, 8, up to 128 for 8 bits. Hex place values are 1 and 16. Denary place values are 1, 10, 100."]],"examples":[["In 01000000 the single 1 is in the second column from the left, so it is worth ",{"answer":"64"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["The same digit is not always worth the same: in denary 700 the 7 is worth seven ",{"answer":"hundreds"},", but in 70 it is only worth seven tens."]]},"wordBank":["64","double","hundreds","position"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Number of values from n bits","windows":{"definition":[["The number of different values (bit patterns) that n bits can represent, which is ",{"answer":"2^n"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Each extra bit ",{"answer":"doubles"}," the number of values. Counting starts at 0, so the largest value is one less: 8 bits give 256 values, 0 to 255."]],"examples":[["4 bits give 2^4 = ",{"answer":"16"}," values. 240 colours need 8 bits, as 7 bits give only 128."]],"nonExamples":[["6 bits do not store numbers up to 64. There are 64 values, but they start at 0, so the maximum is ",{"answer":"63"},"."]]},"wordBank":["16","2^n","63","doubles"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.2/frayer-6.pdf","sourceSha256":"3baeffe161e41c84de9f398a7134af055d334e6a1a1bafe25ecc74d7598989ae","code":"1.2.6","title":"Data storage Characters","terms":[{"term":"Character set","windows":{"definition":[["All the different characters that a computer can represent, where each character has its own ",{"answer":"unique binary code"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Codes follow in order (A = 65, B = 66). More ",{"answer":"bits per character"}," means more characters can be represented. ASCII and Unicode are both character sets."]],"examples":[["In the ASCII character set A is 65 and B is 66, so C is ",{"answer":"67"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Not only letters: a character set also includes digits, punctuation, symbols and the ",{"answer":"space"},". A font is not a character set; it only changes how characters look."]]},"wordBank":["67","bits per character","space","unique binary code"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"ASCII","windows":{"definition":[["A character set that uses ",{"answer":"7 bits"}," per character to represent 128 characters; extended ASCII uses 8 bits to represent 256 characters."]],"characteristics":[["Represents English letters, digits, punctuation and control codes. Upper case letters have consecutive codes. Uses fewer bits per character than Unicode, so text files are ",{"answer":"smaller"},"."]],"examples":[["POP in 8-bit ASCII is stored in order as 01010000 ",{"answer":"01001111"}," 01010000."]],"nonExamples":[["ASCII cannot represent Chinese characters or emojis: it has too few codes. Also 'a' (97) and 'A' (65) have ",{"answer":"different codes"},"; they are not the same character."]]},"wordBank":["01001111","7 bits","different codes","smaller"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Unicode","windows":{"definition":[["A character set that uses more bits per character (16 or more), so it can represent a far ",{"answer":"wider range"}," of characters than ASCII."]],"characteristics":[["Represents characters from many different ",{"answer":"languages"},", plus symbols. Its first 128 codes are the same as ASCII, so ASCII text still works."]],"examples":[["A message mixing English, Arabic and an ",{"answer":"emoji"}," needs Unicode, as ASCII has no codes for them."]],"nonExamples":[["Unicode is not always the better choice: it uses more bits per character, giving a larger ",{"answer":"file size"},". It is not a font; it is a character set."]]},"wordBank":["emoji","file size","languages","wider range"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Character code","windows":{"definition":[["The unique ",{"answer":"binary number"}," given to one character in a character set, so that the character can be stored by the computer."]],"characteristics":[["Letters have ",{"answer":"consecutive"}," codes, so the next letter's code is one more. Every character has a different code. Text is stored as each character's code, one after another."]],"examples":[["If J is 01001010 (hex 4A), K is one more: 01001011, which is hex ",{"answer":"4B"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["The digit character '7' is not stored as the number 7: its ASCII code is ",{"answer":"55"},". Upper and lower case versions of a letter also have different codes."]]},"wordBank":["4B","55","binary number","consecutive"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Bits per character","windows":{"definition":[["The number of bits used to store each character's code, which decides how many ",{"answer":"different characters"}," the character set can represent."]],"characteristics":[["n bits give ",{"answer":"2^n"}," different codes, so 7 bits give 128 codes. More bits per character means more characters can be represented, but a larger file size."]],"examples":[["Extended ASCII uses 8 bits per character, so it can represent 2^8 = ",{"answer":"256"}," characters."]],"nonExamples":[["8 bits per character does not mean only 8 characters can be represented: it means each character's code takes ",{"answer":"1 byte"}," of storage."]]},"wordBank":["1 byte","256","2^n","different characters"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Text file size","windows":{"definition":[["The storage needed for a text file, calculated as bits per character multiplied by the ",{"answer":"number of characters"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Spaces and ",{"answer":"punctuation"}," count as characters. The answer is in bits: divide by 8 for bytes, then by 1000 for kilobytes."]],"examples":[["2000 characters in 8-bit ASCII: 8 x 2000 = 16 000 bits = 2000 bytes = ",{"answer":"2 KB"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Do not add the character codes together (77 + 79 + 80) to find the size: count the characters, then ",{"answer":"multiply"}," by the bits per character."]]},"wordBank":["2 KB","multiply","number of characters","punctuation"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.2/frayer-7.pdf","sourceSha256":"5a6a27c1b21fdbfb3131948038c30e9b240a251647dff230d16bb159e0daca41","code":"1.2.7","title":"Data storage Images","terms":[{"term":"Pixel","windows":{"definition":[["The smallest part of an image: a single square or dot that has ",{"answer":"one colour"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Short for picture element. Pixels are arranged in a grid, and the colour of each pixel is stored as a ",{"answer":"binary code"},". More pixels means more detail and a larger file."]],"examples":[["In a 5 x 5 black and white icon, each of the 25 pixels is stored as ",{"answer":"1 bit"},": 0 = white, 1 = black."]],"nonExamples":[["A pixel cannot show two colours at once. Smooth shading comes from many ",{"answer":"neighbouring pixels"}," of slightly different colours, not from inside one pixel."]]},"wordBank":["1 bit","binary code","neighbouring pixels","one colour"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Colour depth","windows":{"definition":[["The number of ",{"answer":"bits per pixel"},": the number of bits used to store the colour of each pixel."]],"characteristics":[["n bits give 2^n colours, e.g. 2 bits give 4 colours. A higher colour depth means more colours and better quality, but a ",{"answer":"larger file size"},"."]],"examples":[["An image that uses 16 different colours needs a minimum colour depth of ",{"answer":"4 bits"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A colour depth of 8 bits does not mean 8 colours: it gives 2^8 = ",{"answer":"256"}," colours. It is not the number of pixels either; that is the resolution."]]},"wordBank":["256","4 bits","bits per pixel","larger file size"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Resolution","windows":{"definition":[["The number of pixels in an image, given as ",{"answer":"width x height"}," in pixels."]],"characteristics":[["A higher resolution means more pixels, so more ",{"answer":"detail"}," and a sharper image, but more data has to be stored, so the file size is larger."]],"examples":[["An image with a resolution of 800 x 500 contains 800 x 500 = ",{"answer":"400 000"}," pixels."]],"nonExamples":[["Resolution is not the number of colours; that is colour depth. Stretching a small image adds no new pixels, so it just looks ",{"answer":"pixelated"},"."]]},"wordBank":["400 000","detail","pixelated","width x height"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Metadata","windows":{"definition":[["Data ",{"answer":"about the data"},": extra information stored in the image file that describes the image, alongside the pixel data."]],"characteristics":[["Includes width, height, colour depth, file type, date created and geolocation. Without it the computer could not ",{"answer":"reconstruct"}," the image correctly from the binary."]],"examples":[["A photo of 4000 x 3000 pixels using 24 bits per pixel stores 4000, 3000 and ",{"answer":"24"}," as metadata."]],"nonExamples":[["The binary colour codes of the pixels are the ",{"answer":"image data"},", not metadata. Metadata describes the image; it is not the picture itself."]]},"wordBank":["24","about the data","image data","reconstruct"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Image file size","windows":{"definition":[["The storage needed for a bitmap image, calculated as colour depth multiplied by height multiplied by ",{"answer":"width"}," in pixels."]],"characteristics":[["Doubling the colour depth ",{"answer":"doubles"}," the file size. The answer is in bits: divide by 8 for bytes, then by 1000 for kilobytes."]],"examples":[["800 x 500 pixels at 8-bit colour depth: 8 x 500 x 800 = 3 200 000 bits = ",{"answer":"400 KB"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Width x height alone gives the number of ",{"answer":"pixels"},", not the file size: you must also multiply by the colour depth."]]},"wordBank":["400 KB","doubles","pixels","width"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Bitmap image","windows":{"definition":[["An image made of a grid of pixels, where the colour of every pixel is stored as a ",{"answer":"binary number"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Stored pixel by pixel, row by row, ",{"answer":"in order"},". Quality depends on the resolution and colour depth. It becomes pixelated when it is enlarged."]],"examples":[["With 00 = white, 01 = red, 10 = blue and 11 = black, a row stored as 01 01 10 is red, red, ",{"answer":"blue"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A bitmap does not store shapes or lines as drawing instructions. A photo is not stored as one number: ",{"answer":"each pixel"}," has its own binary code."]]},"wordBank":["binary number","blue","each pixel","in order"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.2/frayer-8.pdf","sourceSha256":"ae25ce5303bdd99331353abf9ebcfb154f337acca20b82b68fc7264e4af23792","code":"1.2.8","title":"Data storage Sound","terms":[{"term":"Sample rate","windows":{"definition":[["The number of samples taken ",{"answer":"per second"},", measured in hertz (Hz)."]],"characteristics":[["A higher sample rate means the wave is measured more often, so the recording is ",{"answer":"more accurate"}," (closer to the original sound), but the file size increases."]],"examples":[["A sample rate of 44 kHz means ",{"answer":"44 000"}," samples are taken in every second of the recording."]],"nonExamples":[["Sample rate is not the number of bits in each sample; that is ",{"answer":"bit depth"},". Lowering it from 44 kHz to 8 kHz decreases both the file size and the accuracy."]]},"wordBank":["44 000","bit depth","more accurate","per second"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Bit depth","windows":{"definition":[["The number of ",{"answer":"bits per sample"},": the number of bits used to store each sample of the sound."]],"characteristics":[["A higher bit depth allows a wider range of ",{"answer":"amplitudes"},", so each sample is more accurate and quality improves, but file size increases. n bits = 2^n levels."]],"examples":[["Changing the bit depth from 8 bits to 16 bits raises the number of levels from 256 to ",{"answer":"65 536"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Bit depth is not how often the samples are taken; that is the ",{"answer":"sample rate"},". For images, the matching idea is called colour depth."]]},"wordBank":["65 536","amplitudes","bits per sample","sample rate"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Sampling","windows":{"definition":[["Measuring the ",{"answer":"amplitude"}," (height) of an analogue sound wave at regular intervals so that it can be stored digitally."]],"characteristics":[["Each sample is stored as a binary number, and the samples are stored ",{"answer":"in sequence"},". It converts analogue sound to digital; quality depends on sample rate and bit depth."]],"examples":[["Samples 2, 4, 3, 1 stored with a bit depth of 3 bits become 010, 100, 011, ",{"answer":"001"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Sampling does not measure the frequency or pitch of the wave, and it does not record ",{"answer":"every point"}," on the wave: the values between samples are lost."]]},"wordBank":["001","amplitude","every point","in sequence"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Analogue sound","windows":{"definition":[["Sound in its natural form: a ",{"answer":"continuous"}," wave that changes smoothly, which a computer cannot store directly."]],"characteristics":[["It must be converted to ",{"answer":"digital"}," form by sampling before a computer can store or process it. It can have any value at any moment in time."]],"examples":[["A guitar note travels through the air as an analogue wave until a ",{"answer":"microphone"}," picks it up to be sampled."]],"nonExamples":[["A sound file on a computer is not analogue: its samples only ",{"answer":"approximate"}," the original smooth wave, as the values between samples are lost."]]},"wordBank":["approximate","continuous","digital","microphone"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Duration","windows":{"definition":[["The ",{"answer":"length of time"}," that a sound recording lasts, which must be in seconds for the file size calculation."]],"characteristics":[["A longer duration means ",{"answer":"more samples"}," are stored, so the file size increases, but it has no effect on the quality or accuracy of the sound."]],"examples":[["Recording for 20 minutes instead of 10 ",{"answer":"doubles"}," the file size at the same sample rate and bit depth."]],"nonExamples":[["Duration does not change the quality: only sample rate and bit depth do. Convert minutes to seconds first: 2 minutes is ",{"answer":"120"}," seconds, not 2."]]},"wordBank":["120","doubles","length of time","more samples"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Sound file size","windows":{"definition":[["The storage needed for a sound file, calculated as sample rate multiplied by duration in seconds multiplied by ",{"answer":"bit depth"},"."]],"characteristics":[["The sample rate must be in ",{"answer":"hertz"}," (8 kHz = 8000) and the duration in seconds. The answer is in bits: divide by 8 for bytes. Increasing any value increases the size."]],"examples":[["8000 Hz for 10 seconds at 8 bits: 8000 x 10 x 8 = 640 000 bits = 80 000 bytes = ",{"answer":"80 KB"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["For a rate of 44 kHz multiply by 44 000, not 44. A larger file is not always better quality: a longer ",{"answer":"duration"}," adds size but not accuracy."]]},"wordBank":["80 KB","bit depth","duration","hertz"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.2/frayer-9.pdf","sourceSha256":"ba35178ae5f0169e8d8cd1e245acd1dfa9262eb778e5676eddc2fafc31c4c762","code":"1.2.9","title":"Compression","terms":[{"term":"Compression","windows":{"definition":[["Using an algorithm to reduce the ",{"answer":"file size"},", so that the file is stored using fewer bits."]],"characteristics":[["Smaller files take up less storage space, are faster to upload, download or ",{"answer":"transmit"},", and use less bandwidth. The two types are lossy and lossless."]],"examples":[["A 12 MB file compressed to a third of its size is ",{"answer":"4 MB"},", so it uploads about three times faster."]],"nonExamples":[["Compression does not make the internet connection faster: it means ",{"answer":"less data"}," has to be sent. Moving a file to another drive is not compression either."]]},"wordBank":["4 MB","file size","less data","transmit"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Lossy compression","windows":{"definition":[["Compression that ",{"answer":"permanently"}," removes some data from the file, so the original file cannot be restored."]],"characteristics":[["Usually reduces the file size by ",{"answer":"more than lossless"},". Removes the data the user is least likely to notice, so quality drops slightly. Suits images, sound and video."]],"examples":[["A 10 MB photo for a website is saved with lossy compression as ",{"answer":"1 MB"}," and looks almost the same."]],"nonExamples":[["Never use lossy compression on a text document or program code: every character is needed, so removing data would make the file ",{"answer":"unusable"},"."]]},"wordBank":["1 MB","more than lossless","permanently","unusable"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Lossless compression","windows":{"definition":[["Compression that reduces the file size without losing any data, so the ",{"answer":"original file"}," can be reconstructed exactly."]],"characteristics":[["No data is removed, so there is no loss of quality. Usually reduces the file size by ",{"answer":"less than lossy"},". Needed for text documents, program code and spreadsheets."]],"examples":[["A text document is zipped from 6 MB to 4 MB; unzipping it restores ",{"answer":"every character"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Lossless does not mean the file stays the same size: the file still gets ",{"answer":"smaller"},". It is not only for text; it can be used on images and sound too."]]},"wordBank":["every character","less than lossy","original file","smaller"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.2/frayer-10.pdf","sourceSha256":"3791ae1b2813ba3470d9850674969121a5be330e304b9b30a03df6753de92281","code":"1.2.10","title":"Data storage Numbers","terms":[{"term":"Binary addition","windows":{"definition":[["Adding two binary numbers one column at a time, starting from the least significant bit, using the rules of ",{"answer":"base 2"},"."]],"characteristics":[["0+0 = 0; 0+1 = 1; 1+1 = 10 (write 0, carry 1); 1+1+1 = ",{"answer":"11"}," (write 1, carry 1). An 8-bit answer cannot be more than 255."]],"examples":[["01101110 + 00111001 = ",{"answer":"10100111"}," (110 + 57 = 167)."]],"nonExamples":[["Converting to denary, adding, then converting back is not binary addition. The mark scheme gives the ",{"answer":"method mark"}," for binary columns with carries shown."]]},"wordBank":["10100111","11","base 2","method mark"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Carry","windows":{"definition":[["The 1 that is moved into the next column to the ",{"answer":"left"}," when the total of a column in binary addition is 2 or more."]],"characteristics":[["A total of 2 is binary 10: write 0, carry 1. A total of 3 is binary 11: write 1, carry 1. Carries must be shown in your ",{"answer":"working"}," to get the mark."]],"examples":[["0111 + 0101: the three right-hand columns each produce a carry, giving ",{"answer":"1100"}," (7 + 5 = 12)."]],"nonExamples":[["A carry is not an error. It only causes an ",{"answer":"overflow error"}," when it comes out of the most significant bit and there is no column left to hold it."]]},"wordBank":["1100","left","overflow error","working"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Overflow error","windows":{"definition":[["An error where the result of a binary calculation is too large to be stored in the ",{"answer":"number of bits"}," available."]],"characteristics":[["Adding two 8-bit numbers can give a 9-bit result. The extra carry bit is ",{"answer":"lost"},", so the stored answer is wrong. A left shift can cause it too."]],"examples":[["10010110 + 01110001 (150 + 113 = 263) needs 9 bits: 1 00000111. Only ",{"answer":"00000111"}," is stored."]],"nonExamples":[["An answer of 10100111 (167) is not an overflow: it still fits in 8 bits. Overflow needs a result greater than ",{"answer":"255"},"."]]},"wordBank":["00000111","255","lost","number of bits"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Left binary shift","windows":{"definition":[["Moving every bit to the left by a set number of places; bits pushed off the left end are lost and the right is filled with ",{"answer":"zeros"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Each place shifted ",{"answer":"multiplies"}," the number by 2: 1 place x2, 2 places x4, 3 places x8. If a 1 is lost from the left end, the result is wrong (overflow)."]],"examples":[["00001111 shifted left 4 places gives ",{"answer":"11110000"},": 15 x 16 = 240."]],"nonExamples":[["To multiply by 8 you do not shift 8 places. 2 x 2 x 2 = 8, so the answer is a left shift of ",{"answer":"3 places"},"."]]},"wordBank":["11110000","3 places","multiplies","zeros"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Right binary shift","windows":{"definition":[["Moving every bit to the right by a set number of places; bits pushed off the right end are lost and the left is filled with ",{"answer":"zeros"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Each place shifted ",{"answer":"divides"}," the number by 2: 2 places divides by 4, 3 places by 8. If any 1s drop off the right end, the answer is rounded down."]],"examples":[["11001011 shifted right 2 places gives ",{"answer":"00110010"},": 203 / 4 = 50, remainder lost."]],"nonExamples":[["The answer is not always exact: 203 / 4 is 50.75 but the shift gives 50. The lost 1s cause a loss of ",{"answer":"precision"},", not an overflow."]]},"wordBank":["00110010","divides","precision","zeros"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Most significant bit (MSB)","windows":{"definition":[["The bit in a binary number with the largest place value, which is the ",{"answer":"leftmost"}," bit."]],"characteristics":[["In an 8-bit number the MSB has place value 128. If it is 1 the number is 128 or more. A carry out of the MSB causes an ",{"answer":"overflow"}," error."]],"examples":[["In 10011100 (156) the MSB is the 1 on the far left. Changing it to 0 leaves ",{"answer":"28"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["The MSB is not simply the first 1 you see: in 00011100 the MSB is the leading ",{"answer":"0"}," in the 128 column."]]},"wordBank":["0","28","leftmost","overflow"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Least significant bit (LSB)","windows":{"definition":[["The bit in a binary number with the smallest place value, which is the ",{"answer":"rightmost"}," bit."]],"characteristics":[["The LSB has place value 1. If it is 1 the number is odd; if it is 0 the number is ",{"answer":"even"},". Binary addition always starts at the LSB."]],"examples":[["01001110 is 78. Changing its LSB from 0 to 1 adds 1, which makes ",{"answer":"79"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["The LSB is not unimportant: a wrong LSB changes the answer by 1. It is also the first bit to be lost in a ",{"answer":"right shift"},"."]]},"wordBank":["79","even","right shift","rightmost"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.3/frayer-1.pdf","sourceSha256":"767acb7be66437e9441b08263f51fbc3daf84ee8c37395a486b6efd7136bec08","code":"1.3.1","title":"Networks and topologies","terms":[{"term":"The cloud","windows":{"definition":[[{"answer":"Remote servers"}," that store data and run software, accessed over the internet instead of being held on the user's own computer."]],"characteristics":[["Advantages: access from anywhere with an internet connection; extra storage on demand; provider manages backups and security. Disadvantages: needs internet access; ongoing cost; relies on a ",{"answer":"third party"},"."]],"examples":[["Eve saves her program to cloud storage at school, then opens it at home on a ",{"answer":"different device"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["The cloud is not 'in the air': data sits on a provider's physical servers. It is not risk free: data could be ",{"answer":"hacked"}," or intercepted, and with no internet connection there is no access to the files."]]},"wordBank":["different device","hacked","Remote servers","third party"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Star topology","windows":{"definition":[["A network layout in which every device is connected directly to a central ",{"answer":"switch"}," by its own cable or link."]],"characteristics":[["Easy to add a new node; fewer data ",{"answer":"collisions"},"; if one cable or device fails, the rest of the network still works. Drawback: if the central device fails, the whole network fails."]],"examples":[["Five computers and two printers each connect ",{"answer":"directly"}," to the central device, and to nothing else."]],"nonExamples":[["The centre of a star is not a ",{"answer":"server"},": servers and printers are just nodes plugged into it. If devices also link to each other, giving multiple routes, it is a mesh."]]},"wordBank":["collisions","directly","server","switch"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Mesh topology","windows":{"definition":[["A network layout in which each device is connected to ",{"answer":"every other device"}," (full mesh) or to several other devices (partial mesh), with no central device."]],"characteristics":[["There are ",{"answer":"multiple routes"}," between devices, so there is no single point of failure. Drawback: a large number of connections or cables is needed, which is expensive and impractical."]],"examples":[["Four nodes in a full mesh need ",{"answer":"six"}," links; if one link breaks, data takes another route."]],"nonExamples":[["If every device links only to a central switch it is a ",{"answer":"star"},", not a mesh. Mesh describes how devices are connected; it does not mean peer-to-peer."]]},"wordBank":["every other device","multiple routes","six","star"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Switch","windows":{"definition":[["Hardware that connects devices together in a LAN and sends data only to the intended device, using its ",{"answer":"MAC address"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Receives data from devices; records the addresses of connected devices in a ",{"answer":"table"},"; directs data to the correct destination. If the address is not yet recorded, it sends the data out on all connections."]],"examples":[["A PC prints: the switch sends the data only to the printer, not to ",{"answer":"every device"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A switch does not connect a LAN to the internet: that is the ",{"answer":"router"},", which uses IP addresses to connect networks. A switch works inside one LAN."]]},"wordBank":["every device","MAC address","router","table"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Router","windows":{"definition":[["Hardware that connects different networks together, such as a LAN to a WAN, and forwards ",{"answer":"packets"}," between them."]],"characteristics":[["Receives packets and forwards them using ",{"answer":"IP addresses"},"; maintains a routing table; identifies the most efficient path to the destination; can give each device on the LAN its own address."]],"examples":[["A home router joins the home LAN to the ",{"answer":"internet"}," so a laptop can load a website."]],"nonExamples":[["A router is not a switch: a switch uses ",{"answer":"MAC addresses"}," to connect devices within one LAN, while a router connects networks."]]},"wordBank":["internet","IP addresses","MAC addresses","packets"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Client-server network","windows":{"definition":[["A network in which client computers send ",{"answer":"requests"}," to a central server, which processes each request and responds with data or a service."]],"characteristics":[["Files, security and ",{"answer":"backups"}," are managed centrally on the server. Drawbacks: the server is expensive, needs specialist staff, and if it fails the clients lose access."]],"examples":[["An artist's computer (client) uploads images to a ",{"answer":"web server"},", which stores them and confirms."]],"nonExamples":[["Client-server is not a ",{"answer":"topology"},": it describes the roles of the computers, not how they are connected. A star network can be client-server or peer-to-peer."]]},"wordBank":["backups","requests","topology","web server"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Peer-to-peer network","windows":{"definition":[["A network in which all computers have ",{"answer":"equal status"},": each one can act as both a client and a server, sharing its files and resources directly with the others."]],"characteristics":[["Cheap and easy to set up; no expensive central hardware needed. Drawbacks: no central ",{"answer":"security"}," or backups, each computer is managed separately, and files are unavailable if a peer is switched off."]],"examples":[["Three home PCs share folders and a printer with each other, with ",{"answer":"no server"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Peer-to-peer is not a ",{"answer":"mesh topology"},": it describes how resources are shared, not how devices are connected. It suits small networks, not a large school."]]},"wordBank":["equal status","mesh topology","no server","security"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Domain Name System (DNS)","windows":{"definition":[["A system of servers that converts a website's URL or ",{"answer":"domain name"}," into its matching IP address."]],"characteristics":[["The URL is sent to a DNS server, which looks for it in its ",{"answer":"database"}," of URLs and IP addresses. If not found, the request passes to a higher-level DNS server. The IP (or an error) is returned to the browser."]],"examples":[["First visit to ocr.org.uk: DNS returns the IP address. Next visit: the browser uses the copy saved in its ",{"answer":"cache"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["The DNS does not send the ",{"answer":"web page"},": it only returns the IP address. The browser then uses that IP address to request the page from the web server."]]},"wordBank":["cache","database","domain name","web page"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":26,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":27,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Local area network (LAN)","windows":{"definition":[["A network that covers a ",{"answer":"small geographical area"},", such as a home, a school or an office."]],"characteristics":[["The organisation owns and manages all of its own ",{"answer":"hardware"},": no external infrastructure is used. Devices connect using switches, cables and wireless access points."]],"examples":[["20 PCs in a youth centre's ",{"answer":"single building"}," form a LAN; so does a home network of laptops, phones and TVs."]],"nonExamples":[["A LAN is not defined by the number of computers. Offices in two cities joined together form a ",{"answer":"WAN"},", because the sites are geographically remote."]]},"wordBank":["hardware","single building","small geographical area","WAN"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":29,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":30,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Wide area network (WAN)","windows":{"definition":[["A network that connects computers or LANs over a ",{"answer":"large geographical area"},", such as between cities or countries."]],"characteristics":[["Uses communication media and infrastructure owned by ",{"answer":"third parties"},", such as telephone lines, fibre and satellites, rather than by the organisation itself."]],"examples":[["A bank joins its branch LANs in Leeds and London; the ",{"answer":"internet"}," is the biggest WAN of all."]],"nonExamples":[["A WAN is not defined by the ",{"answer":"number of computers"},": a huge network in one building is still a LAN. A WAN relies on external infrastructure, not only the organisation's own."]]},"wordBank":["internet","large geographical area","number of computers","third parties"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":32,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":33,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Bandwidth","windows":{"definition":[["The maximum amount of ",{"answer":"data"}," that can be transferred over a connection in a given time, measured in bits per second."]],"characteristics":[["Higher bandwidth means faster transfers and better performance. Bandwidth is ",{"answer":"shared"}," between devices, so more users at the same time means less for each and a slower network."]],"examples":[["A 60 Mbit/s link shared equally by three devices gives each about ",{"answer":"20 Mbit/s"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Bandwidth is not the distance a signal travels. It is not the only factor either: performance also depends on number of users, ",{"answer":"interference"}," and the transmission media."]]},"wordBank":["20 Mbit/s","data","interference","shared"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":35,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":36,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Network interface controller (NIC)","windows":{"definition":[["A hardware component inside a device that allows it to ",{"answer":"connect to a network"},", by cable or wirelessly."]],"characteristics":[["Every NIC has a unique ",{"answer":"MAC address"}," set at manufacture. It can be wired or wireless and is usually built into the motherboard. It sends and receives data for its device."]],"examples":[["A desktop's NIC has an ",{"answer":"Ethernet"}," port for a cable; a phone's wireless NIC uses Wi-Fi."]],"nonExamples":[["A NIC is not a ",{"answer":"router"}," or a switch: it only connects its own device to the network and does not forward data between other devices."]]},"wordBank":["connect to a network","Ethernet","MAC address","router"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":38,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":39,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Wireless access point (WAP)","windows":{"definition":[["Hardware that lets wireless devices connect to a wired network using ",{"answer":"radio waves"}," (Wi-Fi)."]],"characteristics":[["Usually connected to a switch or router. Devices must be within ",{"answer":"range"},"; walls and interference weaken the signal. Lets phones and tablets with no Ethernet port join the LAN."]],"examples":[["A ceiling WAP is ",{"answer":"cabled"}," to the school switch, letting 30 tablets join the LAN by Wi-Fi."]],"nonExamples":[["A WAP alone does not give ",{"answer":"internet access"},": it only joins wireless devices to the LAN. A router is still needed to connect the LAN to the internet."]]},"wordBank":["cabled","internet access","radio waves","range"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":41,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":42,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Transmission media","windows":{"definition":[["The channel that carries data signals between devices on a network: either ",{"answer":"wired"}," (cables) or wireless (radio waves)."]],"characteristics":[["Copper Ethernet cable carries electrical signals; fibre optic cable carries light, so is faster over long distances; wireless uses radio waves, which suffer more ",{"answer":"interference"},"."]],"examples":[["A school uses fibre between buildings, copper cable to desktops and ",{"answer":"Wi-Fi"}," for laptops."]],"nonExamples":[["Transmission media are not ",{"answer":"protocols"},": the medium carries the signal, while a protocol is the set of rules for how the data is sent."]]},"wordBank":["interference","protocols","Wi-Fi","wired"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":44,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":45,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Hosting","windows":{"definition":[["Storing a website's files on a ",{"answer":"web server"}," that is always connected to the internet, so that users can access the site."]],"characteristics":[["The host provides storage, a permanent connection, security and backups, usually for a monthly ",{"answer":"fee"},". The server answers clients' browsers at any time of day or night."]],"examples":[["An artist uploads images to a hosting company; visitors' browsers ",{"answer":"request"}," the pages."]],"nonExamples":[["Hosting is not the same as owning a ",{"answer":"domain name"},": the name is the address, hosting is the server storing the files. A home PC that gets switched off is a poor host."]]},"wordBank":["domain name","fee","request","web server"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":47,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":48,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"The internet","windows":{"definition":[["A global ",{"answer":"network of networks"},": a worldwide collection of interconnected computer networks (the largest WAN)."]],"characteristics":[["Packets are routed to their destination using ",{"answer":"IP addresses"},". It carries many services, such as web pages, email and file transfer. No single organisation owns it."]],"examples":[["A laptop in Hull loads a page from a server in the USA: the packets pass through ",{"answer":"many routers"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["The internet is not the ",{"answer":"World Wide Web"},": the web is the collection of web pages, just one service that runs over the internet's hardware."]]},"wordBank":["IP addresses","many routers","network of networks","World Wide Web"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":50,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":51,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.3/frayer-2.pdf","sourceSha256":"7e2fab1aaa6653942226e50486f548786148fe9e51353d81722f39e487e6284a","code":"1.3.2","title":"Wired and wireless networks, protocols and layers","terms":[{"term":"IP address","windows":{"definition":[["A unique address given to a device on a network, used to ",{"answer":"route"}," data to it across networks and the internet."]],"characteristics":[["IPv4: four denary numbers from 0 to ",{"answer":"255"}," separated by full stops. IPv6: eight groups of hex numbers (0 to FFFF) separated by colons. Allocated by the network, so it can change."]],"examples":[["192.154.21.2 is IPv4. 0252:5985:89ab:cdde:a57f:89ad:efcd:00fe is ",{"answer":"IPv6"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["258.0.0.3 is invalid (258 is too big) and 56.1.2.66.1 is invalid (",{"answer":"five"}," numbers). An IP address is not fixed to the hardware: that is the MAC address."]]},"wordBank":["255","five","IPv6","route"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"MAC address","windows":{"definition":[["A unique identifier given to a device's ",{"answer":"network interface controller"}," when it is made; it does not change."]],"characteristics":[["Written as six pairs of ",{"answer":"hexadecimal"}," digits separated by colons or hyphens (12 digits, 48 bits). The first half identifies the manufacturer; the second half is the serial number."]],"examples":[["00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E. A ",{"answer":"switch"}," uses MAC addresses to send data to the right device on a LAN."]],"nonExamples":[["A MAC address is not an ",{"answer":"IP address"},": an IP address can change when the device joins another network and is used for routing between networks; the MAC address stays the same."]]},"wordBank":["hexadecimal","IP address","network interface controller","switch"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Protocol","windows":{"definition":[["A set of ",{"answer":"rules"}," for how data is transmitted and how devices communicate across a network."]],"characteristics":[["They cover things such as data format, addressing and ",{"answer":"error checking"},". Both devices must use the same protocol. Different protocols do different jobs."]],"examples":[["A browser and a web server both follow HTTP, so each ",{"answer":"understands"}," the other's messages."]],"nonExamples":[["A protocol is not hardware or a ",{"answer":"cable"},": the cable carries the signal; the protocol decides the format and order of the messages the devices send."]]},"wordBank":["cable","error checking","rules","understands"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Layers","windows":{"definition":[["A division of network ",{"answer":"functionality"},": each layer is a group of protocols that handles one part of the communication."]],"characteristics":[["Each layer is ",{"answer":"self-contained"},": it can be changed or replaced without affecting the others. Developers can specialise in one layer, and it promotes compatibility between manufacturers."]],"examples":[["Swapping Ethernet for Wi-Fi changes only ",{"answer":"one layer"},": HTTP in the browser still works."]],"nonExamples":[["Layers are not physical ",{"answer":"hardware"}," stacked inside a computer. One layer does not need to know how the others work; it just passes data to the layer above or below."]]},"wordBank":["functionality","hardware","one layer","self-contained"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Ethernet","windows":{"definition":[["A standard for ",{"answer":"wired"}," connections in a LAN, setting out how data is transmitted between devices."]],"characteristics":[["High, consistent ",{"answer":"bandwidth"},"; little interference, so reliable; more secure, as a physical connection is needed. Drawbacks: devices are fixed in place and cables cost money to install."]],"examples":[["An airport check-in PC is plugged into a ",{"answer":"switch"}," for a fast, reliable, secure connection."]],"nonExamples":[["Ethernet is not the ",{"answer":"internet"},": it connects devices within a LAN. Connecting with radio waves instead is Wi-Fi, not Ethernet."]]},"wordBank":["bandwidth","internet","switch","wired"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Wi-Fi","windows":{"definition":[["A standard for wireless connections in a LAN, transmitting data using ",{"answer":"radio waves"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Devices can move around within ",{"answer":"range"},"; easy to add devices; no trailing cables. Drawbacks: prone to interference, slower, and less secure, so data should be encrypted."]],"examples":[["A laptop stays connected through a wireless access point while ",{"answer":"moving"}," between rooms."]],"nonExamples":[["Wi-Fi is not the internet: a device can have a Wi-Fi link to the LAN with no internet access. It is not ",{"answer":"Bluetooth"},", which links two devices over a very short range."]]},"wordBank":["Bluetooth","moving","radio waves","range"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Encryption","windows":{"definition":[["Scrambling data using a key so that it cannot be ",{"answer":"understood"}," by anyone who does not have the key."]],"characteristics":[["Plaintext is converted to ",{"answer":"ciphertext"},"; only a device with the correct key can decrypt it. Vital on wireless networks, where signals can be picked up by anyone in range."]],"examples":[["A Caesar shift of 3 turns CAT into ",{"answer":"FDW"},"; Wi-Fi and HTTPS use far stronger encryption."]],"nonExamples":[["Encryption does not stop data being ",{"answer":"intercepted"}," or stolen: it makes the stolen data meaningless. It is not the same as compression or a password."]]},"wordBank":["ciphertext","FDW","intercepted","understood"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Standard","windows":{"definition":[["A set of specifications for hardware or software that is followed by all ",{"answer":"manufacturers"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Standards give ",{"answer":"compatibility"},": hardware and software from different makers can work together. Ethernet is a standard because it is used in almost every networked device."]],"examples":[["Laptops and phones from different makers all join one network, as each follows the ",{"answer":"Wi-Fi"}," standard."]],"nonExamples":[["A standard is not a law, and not one company's private design: it is ",{"answer":"agreed"}," across the industry. A protocol is rules for communication; a standard can cover any hardware or software."]]},"wordBank":["agreed","compatibility","manufacturers","Wi-Fi"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":26,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":27,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"TCP/IP","windows":{"definition":[["Transmission Control Protocol / Internet Protocol: a ",{"answer":"suite of protocols"},", arranged in layers, that controls how data is sent across the internet."]],"characteristics":[["TCP splits data into packets, numbers them, checks they arrive and ",{"answer":"reassembles"}," them in order. IP adds the addresses and routes each packet to its destination."]],"examples":[["A photo is sent as numbered packets; a missing packet is ",{"answer":"resent"}," and all are put back in order."]],"nonExamples":[["TCP/IP is not only used for loading ",{"answer":"web pages"},": email and file transfer use it too. IP alone does not put packets back in order: that is TCP's job."]]},"wordBank":["reassembles","resent","suite of protocols","web pages"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":29,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":30,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"HTTP","windows":{"definition":[["Hypertext Transfer Protocol: used by a browser to ",{"answer":"request"}," web pages from a web server, which sends them back."]],"characteristics":[["Works in a client-server way: the browser asks and the server replies with the page. Data is sent as ",{"answer":"plain text"},", so anyone who intercepts it can read it."]],"examples":[["Visiting http://www.example.com loads a public news page; nothing ",{"answer":"private"}," is being sent."]],"nonExamples":[["HTTP is not secure: never use it for passwords or bank details, which need ",{"answer":"HTTPS"},". It is not a language for writing pages: that is HTML."]]},"wordBank":["HTTPS","plain text","private","request"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":32,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":33,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"HTTPS","windows":{"definition":[["Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure: transfers web pages like HTTP, but the data is ",{"answer":"encrypted"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Data cannot be understood if it is intercepted. The browser shows a padlock. Used whenever ",{"answer":"personal"}," or payment data is sent. The web server is also authenticated."]],"examples":[["Entering a username and password to access a ",{"answer":"bank account"}," online uses HTTPS."]],"nonExamples":[["HTTPS does not stop data being intercepted, and does not prove a website is ",{"answer":"trustworthy"},": fake sites can use it too. It only protects data while it is being transmitted."]]},"wordBank":["bank account","encrypted","personal","trustworthy"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":35,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":36,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"FTP","windows":{"definition":[["File Transfer Protocol: used to ",{"answer":"upload"}," and download files between a client and a server over a network."]],"characteristics":[["Uses a client-server model; users often ",{"answer":"log in"}," with a username and password. Good for large files and whole folders of files. Standard FTP is not encrypted."]],"examples":[["A web designer uses FTP to move a site's HTML files and images onto the ",{"answer":"web server"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["FTP is not for email or for viewing web pages: those use SMTP and ",{"answer":"HTTP"},". FTP moves files; it does not display them."]]},"wordBank":["HTTP","log in","upload","web server"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":38,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":39,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"SMTP","windows":{"definition":[["Simple Mail Transfer Protocol: used to ",{"answer":"send"}," emails from a client to a mail server, and between mail servers."]],"characteristics":[["The email is passed from server to server until it reaches the recipient's mail server, where it waits in their ",{"answer":"inbox"}," to be collected using POP or IMAP."]],"examples":[["Ali presses Send: SMTP carries this ",{"answer":"outgoing"}," email from his app to his mail server, then on to Bea's."]],"nonExamples":[["SMTP does not ",{"answer":"retrieve"}," email for you: checking an inbox for new messages uses POP or IMAP."]]},"wordBank":["inbox","outgoing","retrieve","send"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":41,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":42,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"POP","windows":{"definition":[["Post Office Protocol: used to ",{"answer":"download"}," emails from a mail server onto the user's device."]],"characteristics":[["Once downloaded, the email is usually ",{"answer":"deleted"}," from the server, so it can only be read on that one device. Emails can then be read offline, and server space is freed."]],"examples":[["Emails downloaded to a home PC using POP are then ",{"answer":"not available"}," on the user's phone."]],"nonExamples":[["POP does not send email (that is SMTP) and does not ",{"answer":"synchronise"}," devices (that is IMAP). POP and IMAP both retrieve email; they differ in where it is kept."]]},"wordBank":["deleted","download","not available","synchronise"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":44,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":45,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"IMAP","windows":{"definition":[["Internet Message Access Protocol: used to read emails that stay ",{"answer":"on the mail server"},", rather than downloading and deleting them."]],"characteristics":[["Emails and folders are ",{"answer":"synchronised"}," across all the user's devices: read on one, marked as read on all. Needs an internet connection and uses server storage space."]],"examples":[["An email read on a phone shows as read on the laptop too, as both view the ",{"answer":"same mailbox"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["IMAP does not send email: that is ",{"answer":"SMTP"},". Unlike POP, it does not remove the message from the server once it has been read."]]},"wordBank":["on the mail server","same mailbox","SMTP","synchronised"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":47,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":48,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Bluetooth","windows":{"definition":[["A wireless standard that uses radio waves to connect two devices directly over a very ",{"answer":"short range"},", usually under 10 metres."]],"characteristics":[["Devices must be ",{"answer":"paired"}," first. Low power, so good for battery devices, but low bandwidth, so slow. Needs no router or wireless access point."]],"examples":[["A phone streams ",{"answer":"music"}," to wireless headphones a few metres away."]],"nonExamples":[["Bluetooth is not for networking a whole building or for internet access: that is ",{"answer":"Wi-Fi"},", which has a longer range and higher bandwidth."]]},"wordBank":["music","paired","short range","Wi-Fi"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":50,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":51,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.4/frayer-1.pdf","sourceSha256":"72a5416f334f4f97f0b4c03860d9ed4c661f539ac7f779c4ee634bb0f84da62d","code":"1.4.1","title":"Threats to computer systems and networks","terms":[{"term":"Malware","windows":{"definition":[[{"answer":"Malicious software"}," that is installed without the user's knowledge in order to damage, delete, corrupt or steal data."]],"characteristics":[["Types: a virus replicates by attaching itself to files; a ",{"answer":"worm"}," replicates across a network; a trojan is disguised as legitimate software; spyware secretly records what the user does."]],"examples":[["A user opens an email attachment. ",{"answer":"Ransomware"}," encrypts their files and demands a fee to unlock them."]],"nonExamples":[["A software bug that crashes a program is not malware: it was not written to cause harm. Malware is limited by ",{"answer":"anti-malware software"},", which scans for it and removes it."]]},"wordBank":["anti-malware software","Malicious software","Ransomware","worm"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Phishing","windows":{"definition":[["Sending a fake email or message that pretends to be from a ",{"answer":"trusted organisation"}," to trick the user into giving away personal data."]],"characteristics":[["Usually contains a ",{"answer":"link"}," to a fake website where the user enters login or bank details. Purpose: to steal personal data for fraud or identity theft. It is a form of social engineering."]],"examples":[["An email 'from your bank' says your account is locked and asks you to log in via a link to a ",{"answer":"fake website"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Guessing a password with software is brute force, not phishing. Phishing tricks a person, so it is limited by ",{"answer":"training users"}," to spot fake emails and by email filters, not by strong passwords."]]},"wordBank":["fake website","link","training users","trusted organisation"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Social engineering","windows":{"definition":[["Manipulating or deceiving ",{"answer":"people"}," into giving away confidential data or access to a computer system."]],"characteristics":[["Exploits human error, not a technical weakness: people are the ",{"answer":"weak point"}," of a system. Forms include phishing, fake phone calls and shoulder surfing (watching a password being typed)."]],"examples":[["A caller pretends to be an IT technician and persuades a receptionist to reveal their ",{"answer":"password"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A flood of requests crashing a server is a DoS attack: no person is tricked. Social engineering cannot be stopped by a firewall; it is limited by ",{"answer":"staff training"}," and network policies."]]},"wordBank":["password","people","staff training","weak point"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Brute-force attack","windows":{"definition":[["An attack that uses software to try every possible ",{"answer":"combination"}," of characters, by trial and error, until the correct password is found."]],"characteristics":[["Automated, so thousands of guesses are made every second. Purpose: to gain ",{"answer":"unauthorised access"}," to an account or system. Short, simple passwords are cracked fastest."]],"examples":[["Software tries 0000, 0001, 0002 and so on until it finds a 4-digit PIN: at most ",{"answer":"10 000"}," attempts."]],"nonExamples":[["Tricking a user into revealing a password is phishing, not brute force. Brute force is limited by strong passwords, a firewall and a limit on the number of ",{"answer":"login attempts"},"."]]},"wordBank":["10 000","combination","login attempts","unauthorised access"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Denial of service (DoS) attack","windows":{"definition":[["An attack that floods a server with so many ",{"answer":"requests"}," that it cannot respond to them all."]],"characteristics":[["Uses up all the server's bandwidth or processing, so it slows down or crashes. Purpose: to deny ",{"answer":"legitimate users"}," access to a service. A DDoS attack uses many infected computers at once."]],"examples":[["Thousands of infected computers send requests to a ticket website, so the server ",{"answer":"crashes"}," and fans cannot buy tickets."]],"nonExamples":[["A DoS attack does not steal or read data; that is data interception. It only makes a service unavailable. It is limited by a ",{"answer":"firewall"}," that blocks traffic from suspicious addresses."]]},"wordBank":["crashes","firewall","legitimate users","requests"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"SQL injection","windows":{"definition":[["Entering ",{"answer":"SQL code"}," into a website input box, such as a login form, so that the database runs it as a command."]],"characteristics":[["Works when input is not checked (validated) before it is used in a query. Purpose: to view, change or ",{"answer":"delete"}," data in the database, or to bypass a login, without authorisation."]],"examples":[["Typing ' OR '1'='1 into a password box makes the query's condition always ",{"answer":"true"},", so the attacker is logged in."]],"nonExamples":[["Typing thousands of different passwords into a login form is brute force, not SQL injection. SQL injection is limited by input validation, and the weakness is found by ",{"answer":"penetration testing"},"."]]},"wordBank":["delete","penetration testing","SQL code","true"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Data interception and theft","windows":{"definition":[["Data being read or copied by an ",{"answer":"unauthorised"}," person or program while it is being transmitted across a network."]],"characteristics":[["Software called a packet sniffer examines ",{"answer":"packets"}," as they travel. Purpose: to steal logins, bank details or personal data. Wireless networks are at greater risk than wired ones."]],"examples":[["On public Wi-Fi in a coffee shop, an attacker captures the ",{"answer":"unencrypted"}," card details a shopper sends to a website."]],"nonExamples":[["A firewall or penetration test does not stop data being intercepted in transit. Only ",{"answer":"encryption"}," limits this threat: the data can still be captured but it cannot be understood."]]},"wordBank":["encryption","packets","unauthorised","unencrypted"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.4/frayer-2.pdf","sourceSha256":"4078810a92cbf31295ead5e003bfbf159f83065e089e09ab8a2799b9942da740","code":"1.4.2","title":"Identifying and preventing vulnerabilities","terms":[{"term":"Firewall","windows":{"definition":[["Hardware or software that monitors ",{"answer":"incoming and outgoing"}," network traffic and blocks any that does not meet its rules."]],"characteristics":[["Examines packets against a set of rules: it can block by ",{"answer":"IP address"}," or port. Helps prevent unauthorised access by hackers, DoS attacks, brute-force attacks and malware entering the network."]],"examples":[["A school firewall ",{"answer":"blocks"}," traffic from a suspicious address but allows pupils' requests to approved websites."]],"nonExamples":[["A firewall does not scramble data, so it cannot stop data being read in transit: that needs ",{"answer":"encryption"},". It also cannot stop a user giving details away in reply to a phishing email."]]},"wordBank":["blocks","encryption","incoming and outgoing","IP address"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Encryption","windows":{"definition":[["Scrambling data using an algorithm and a ",{"answer":"key"}," so that it cannot be understood if it is intercepted."]],"characteristics":[["Plaintext becomes ",{"answer":"ciphertext"},"; only someone with the correct key can decrypt it. It limits data interception and theft: it does not stop data being intercepted, but it cannot be understood."]],"examples":[["A website using HTTPS encrypts card details, so a hacker who intercepts them sees only ",{"answer":"meaningless"}," characters."]],"nonExamples":[["Encryption does not remove malware or block a DoS attack, and it does not make files smaller: that is ",{"answer":"compression"},". Encrypted data can still be deleted or stolen; it just cannot be read."]]},"wordBank":["ciphertext","compression","key","meaningless"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Anti-malware software","windows":{"definition":[["Software that scans a computer's files to ",{"answer":"detect"}," malware such as viruses and spyware, then quarantines or deletes it."]],"characteristics":[["Compares files with a database of known malware ",{"answer":"signatures"},", so it must be updated regularly. Scans downloads and attachments as they arrive. Limits viruses, worms, trojans, spyware, ransomware."]],"examples":[["A downloaded game contains a trojan. The scanner finds a match and moves the file to ",{"answer":"quarantine"}," so it cannot run."]],"nonExamples":[["It does not block network traffic using rules; that is a ",{"answer":"firewall"},". It cannot stop phishing, brute-force attacks or data interception, and it misses new malware if it is not updated."]]},"wordBank":["detect","firewall","quarantine","signatures"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Penetration testing","windows":{"definition":[["An authorised person deliberately tries to break into a system in order to identify its ",{"answer":"vulnerabilities"}," before real attackers do."]],"characteristics":[["The tester has ",{"answer":"permission"}," and reports the weaknesses found so they can be fixed. Helps limit SQL injection, brute-force attacks and other hacking by finding the holes first."]],"examples":[["A bank pays an ethical hacker to attack its website. The ",{"answer":"report"}," shows a login form open to SQL injection."]],"nonExamples":[["Attacking a system without permission is illegal hacking, not penetration testing. A pen test only finds weaknesses; it does not fix them, and it cannot prevent ",{"answer":"data interception"},"."]]},"wordBank":["data interception","permission","report","vulnerabilities"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"User access levels","windows":{"definition":[["Rules that control which data and software each user can access, and whether they can ",{"answer":"read, edit or delete"}," it."]],"characteristics":[["Users get only the access their job needs, e.g. ",{"answer":"read-only"}," or read-write. Limits the damage from insiders, human error, malware and a hacked account, because less data can be reached."]],"examples":[["A pupil can read files in a shared folder; a teacher can also edit them; only the ",{"answer":"administrator"}," can install software."]],"nonExamples":[["Access levels do not check who a user is: that is done with a ",{"answer":"password"},". They decide what a logged-in user may do, and they cannot stop data being intercepted."]]},"wordBank":["administrator","password","read, edit or delete","read-only"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Passwords","windows":{"definition":[["A secret string of characters, used with a username, to ",{"answer":"authenticate"}," a user: it proves they are allowed to access the account."]],"characteristics":[["A strong password is long and mixes upper case, lower case, numbers and ",{"answer":"symbols"},". It should be changed regularly and never shared. Limits brute-force attacks and unauthorised access."]],"examples":[["T7#rq!Lm92xP is strong; 'password1' is weak. The account locks after ",{"answer":"three"}," wrong attempts."]],"nonExamples":[["A strong password does not help if the user types it into a fake website: that is ",{"answer":"phishing"},". Passwords also do not protect data while it is being transmitted."]]},"wordBank":["authenticate","phishing","symbols","three"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Physical security","windows":{"definition":[["Measures that stop unauthorised people from physically reaching computer ",{"answer":"hardware"},", such as servers, cables and backups."]],"characteristics":[["Examples: locked doors, key cards, ",{"answer":"biometrics"},", CCTV, alarms and security guards. Limits theft of devices and data, damage to hardware, and people plugging in infected USB drives."]],"examples":[["A hospital keeps its servers in a locked room that opens only with a staff ",{"answer":"key card"},"; CCTV records who enters."]],"nonExamples":[["A lock cannot stop attacks that arrive over the network, such as a DoS attack or SQL injection: those need a ",{"answer":"firewall"}," or penetration testing. It only protects against people on site."]]},"wordBank":["biometrics","firewall","hardware","key card"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.5/frayer-1.pdf","sourceSha256":"cb865e03c992d0c36a017244a007c39a25de59c3efee121f3d21e5839623160a","code":"1.5.1","title":"Operating systems","terms":[{"term":"Operating system","windows":{"definition":[[{"answer":"System software"}," that manages the computer's hardware and provides a platform for application software to run on."]],"characteristics":[["Functions: user interface, memory management and multitasking, peripheral management and drivers, ",{"answer":"user management"},", file management. e.g. Windows, macOS, Android."]],"examples":[["Opening a game: the OS loads it into memory, reads the controller using a ",{"answer":"driver"}," and shows output on screen."]],"nonExamples":[["A word processor or web browser is ",{"answer":"application software"},", not an OS: it does a task for the user. Defragmentation software is a utility, which is separate from the OS functions."]]},"wordBank":["application software","driver","System software","user management"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Memory management","windows":{"definition":[["The OS function that allocates ",{"answer":"RAM"}," to each running program and moves data between secondary storage and main memory."]],"characteristics":[["Stops programs overwriting each other's data and frees memory when a program closes. When RAM is full it moves data to and from ",{"answer":"virtual memory"},". It makes multitasking possible."]],"examples":[["With a browser and a game open, each gets its own ",{"answer":"block"}," of RAM; closing the game frees its space."]],"nonExamples":[["Creating, renaming or deleting a folder on the hard disk is ",{"answer":"file management"},". Memory management deals with RAM (primary storage), not with how files are organised on secondary storage."]]},"wordBank":["block","file management","RAM","virtual memory"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Multitasking","windows":{"definition":[["Running more than one program at what appears to be the same time, by the OS sharing ",{"answer":"processor time"}," between them."]],"characteristics":[["The CPU switches between tasks so ",{"answer":"quickly"}," that they seem simultaneous. OCR treats multitasking as part of memory management: every running program is held in RAM at once."]],"examples":[["A student streams music, types an essay and downloads a file; the OS gives each a ",{"answer":"time slice"}," in turn."]],"nonExamples":[["A single core does not really execute several programs at the same instant; it ",{"answer":"switches"}," between them. Having many files saved on a disk is not multitasking: the programs must be running."]]},"wordBank":["processor time","quickly","switches","time slice"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Peripheral management","windows":{"definition":[["The OS function that controls communication between the computer and its ",{"answer":"input and output"}," devices."]],"characteristics":[["Receives data from input devices and sends data to output devices, using ",{"answer":"device drivers"},". Peripherals are external hardware such as a printer, keyboard, mouse or scanner."]],"examples":[["A user scans a photo: the OS reads the data from the ",{"answer":"scanner"},", then later sends the image to a printer."]],"nonExamples":[["Creating user accounts is ",{"answer":"user management"},", and moving data into RAM is memory management: RAM is internal, not a peripheral. Peripheral management only deals with attached devices."]]},"wordBank":["device drivers","input and output","scanner","user management"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Device driver","windows":{"definition":[["A program that allows the operating system to ",{"answer":"communicate"}," with a specific piece of hardware."]],"characteristics":[["Translates OS instructions into ",{"answer":"signals"}," that the device understands. Each device needs its own driver, often supplied by the manufacturer and updated to fix bugs or add features."]],"examples":[["Plugging in a new printer: the OS installs the ",{"answer":"printer driver"}," before the first page can be printed."]],"nonExamples":[["A driver is ",{"answer":"software"},", not the cable or port that connects the device. One driver will not work for every device: a graphics card driver cannot control a printer."]]},"wordBank":["communicate","printer driver","signals","software"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"User interface","windows":{"definition":[["The part of the OS that allows the user to ",{"answer":"interact"}," with the computer, by giving input and receiving output."]],"characteristics":[["A GUI uses windows, ",{"answer":"icons"},", menus and pointers (WIMP), so it is easy for beginners. A command line interface (CLI) uses typed commands: it needs less memory but commands must be learnt."]],"examples":[["To delete a file, a GUI user drags it to the bin; a CLI user types a ",{"answer":"command"}," such as del notes.txt."]],"nonExamples":[["The monitor and mouse are ",{"answer":"hardware"},", not the user interface. The interface is the software that takes the user's input and shows output, such as a desktop or a command prompt."]]},"wordBank":["command","hardware","icons","interact"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"User management","windows":{"definition":[["The OS function that creates and controls ",{"answer":"user accounts"},", so that several people can share one computer securely."]],"characteristics":[["Each user logs in with a username and ",{"answer":"password"},". The OS sets access rights for each account, keeps each user's files and settings separate, and lets an administrator add or delete users."]],"examples":[["On a family PC a parent has an administrator account; a child's account cannot ",{"answer":"install software"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Renaming or moving a user's documents is ",{"answer":"file management"},". User management covers accounts, logins, passwords and access rights, not what is stored inside the files."]]},"wordBank":["file management","install software","password","user accounts"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"File management","windows":{"definition":[["The OS function that organises files into ",{"answer":"folders"}," on secondary storage and keeps track of where each file is saved."]],"characteristics":[["Lets the user save, copy and ",{"answer":"rename"}," files and folders. It keeps track of file extensions and sets permissions, such as read-only, for each file."]],"examples":[["A student creates a folder called Homework, moves essay.docx into it and ",{"answer":"deletes"}," an old draft."]],"nonExamples":[["Moving data from virtual memory to RAM is ",{"answer":"memory management"},", not file management. Rearranging the fragments of files on a hard disk is done by defragmentation utility software."]]},"wordBank":["deletes","folders","memory management","rename"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":26,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":27,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.5/frayer-2.pdf","sourceSha256":"b322010c7079041765dca1e60cf98bfd7c4a9e3230456ba8e96c7ffcfe6e4ce1","code":"1.5.2","title":"Utility software","terms":[{"term":"Utility software","windows":{"definition":[["System software that performs ",{"answer":"housekeeping"}," or maintenance tasks to keep a computer running smoothly and efficiently."]],"characteristics":[["Each utility does one specific job to ",{"answer":"maintain"},", manage or protect the system, working alongside the operating system. J277 utilities: encryption, defragmentation and data compression software."]],"examples":[["Before emailing coursework, a student uses a ",{"answer":"compression"}," utility to zip the folder so it sends faster."]],"nonExamples":[["A spreadsheet or game is ",{"answer":"application software"},": it does a task for the user, not for the system. In the exam, naming one utility is not enough when asked for the purpose of utility software."]]},"wordBank":["application software","compression","housekeeping","maintain"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Defragmentation","windows":{"definition":[["Utility software that moves the separated parts of files on a hard disk so that each file is stored in ",{"answer":"consecutive"}," locations."]],"characteristics":[["Free space is also ",{"answer":"grouped together"},", so new files are less likely to be split. Files are read faster because the read/write head moves less. Only useful on magnetic hard disks."]],"examples":[["A restaurant's slow ordering system has its ",{"answer":"hard disk"}," defragmented; order files then open faster."]],"nonExamples":[["An ",{"answer":"SSD"}," does not need defragmenting: it has no moving read/write head, so access is equally fast anywhere, and extra writes wear it out. Defragmenting does not delete files or add capacity."]]},"wordBank":["consecutive","grouped together","hard disk","SSD"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Fragmentation","windows":{"definition":[["When the parts of a file are stored in ",{"answer":"separate"}," locations on a hard disk instead of next to each other."]],"characteristics":[["Happens over time: files are deleted, leaving ",{"answer":"gaps"},"; a new larger file does not fit in one gap, so it is split. The read/write head must move further, so files take longer to load."]],"examples":[["A 3-block file is deleted. A 5-block video V is saved: 3 blocks fill the gap and ",{"answer":"2"}," go elsewhere."]],"nonExamples":[["Fragmentation does not delete or corrupt data: all the parts still exist and the file opens normally, only more ",{"answer":"slowly"},". It is not a problem on SSDs, which have no moving parts."]]},"wordBank":["2","gaps","separate","slowly"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Encryption software","windows":{"definition":[["Utility software that uses an algorithm and a key to ",{"answer":"scramble"}," data so that it cannot be understood by unauthorised users."]],"characteristics":[["The data can only be read after it is decrypted with the correct ",{"answer":"key"},". If encrypted data is intercepted or stolen it is meaningless. It does not stop the data from being intercepted."]],"examples":[["A teacher encrypts a USB stick of pupil data. If it is lost, the finder cannot ",{"answer":"understand"}," the files."]],"nonExamples":[["Encryption does not reduce file size: that is ",{"answer":"compression"}," software. It does not stop a file being deleted or copied, and it does not remove malware from the file."]]},"wordBank":["compression","key","scramble","understand"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Data compression software","windows":{"definition":[["Utility software that uses an algorithm to reduce the ",{"answer":"file size"}," of data so that it takes up less storage space."]],"characteristics":[["Smaller files are quicker to ",{"answer":"transmit"}," over a network or attach to an email. Lossy permanently removes data; lossless lets the original be restored exactly, e.g. a zip file."]],"examples":[["A 20 MB folder of documents is zipped to 5 MB with ",{"answer":"lossless"}," compression, then unzipped with nothing lost."]],"nonExamples":[["Compression does not keep data secret: that is ",{"answer":"encryption"}," software. Defragmentation does not shrink files either, it only rearranges them. Lossy must not be used on text or program files."]]},"wordBank":["encryption","file size","lossless","transmit"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/1.6/frayer-1.pdf","sourceSha256":"12cd970a8a1c54936b474938ed2aef1543ddf36a7f3257f28fd1a0ea398503f5","code":"1.6.1","title":"Ethical, legal, cultural and environmental impacts","terms":[{"term":"Data Protection Act 2018","windows":{"definition":[["UK law that sets rules for how organisations collect, store and use ",{"answer":"personal data"},", and gives rights to the people the data is about (data subjects)."]],"characteristics":[["Data must be used fairly and lawfully, only for the stated purpose, kept accurate and up to date, kept ",{"answer":"secure"}," and deleted when no longer needed. Data subjects can ask to see their data."]],"examples":[["A youth centre stores members' addresses: it collects only ",{"answer":"relevant data"}," and does not share it without consent."]],"nonExamples":[["Guessing a password to read someone's records breaks the ",{"answer":"Computer Misuse Act"},"; the DPA covers how the organisation holding the data looks after it."]]},"wordBank":["Computer Misuse Act","personal data","relevant data","secure"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Computer Misuse Act 1990","windows":{"definition":[["UK law that makes it a criminal offence to gain ",{"answer":"unauthorised access"}," to computer systems or data, or to modify data without permission."]],"characteristics":[["Offences include: unauthorised access (hacking); unauthorised access with ",{"answer":"intent"}," to commit further crime; unauthorised modification of data, such as planting a virus or deleting files."]],"examples":[["Guessing a teacher's password to log in to their account, or installing a ",{"answer":"key logger"}," on an office computer."]],"nonExamples":[["Copying and sharing a movie without permission breaks ",{"answer":"copyright law"}," (CDPA 1988), not this Act. Logging in to your own account is authorised access."]]},"wordBank":["copyright law","intent","key logger","unauthorised access"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988","windows":{"definition":[["UK law that protects creators' original work from being ",{"answer":"copied"}," or distributed without the permission of the copyright owner."]],"characteristics":[["Covers software, images, music, video and text. Protection is automatic; others need permission or a ",{"answer":"licence"}," to use the work. Breaking it can lead to fines or prison."]],"examples":[["Layla scans her hand-drawn images; this Act makes it illegal for others to copy or ",{"answer":"share"}," them without permission."]],"nonExamples":[["It does not protect personal data (that is the ",{"answer":"Data Protection Act"},") and it does not make hacking illegal. Open source code is still covered by copyright."]]},"wordBank":["copied","Data Protection Act","licence","share"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Open source software","windows":{"definition":[["Software whose ",{"answer":"source code"}," is made available so that users can view it, modify it and redistribute it."]],"characteristics":[["Usually free of charge. Users can tailor it to their needs and fix bugs, and a community improves it. Usually no official ",{"answer":"support"},", and the creator earns little money."]],"examples":[["Eve releases her game as open source: customers get it free and can ",{"answer":"add features"}," by editing the code."]],"nonExamples":[["Open source does not mean copyright free: it still has a ",{"answer":"licence"},". Software that is free to use but whose code is hidden is not open source."]]},"wordBank":["add features","licence","source code","support"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Proprietary software","windows":{"definition":[["Software owned by a person or company and distributed under a licence, usually for a fee, where the source code is ",{"answer":"not available"}," to users."]],"characteristics":[["Users usually pay; they cannot legally modify, copy or ",{"answer":"redistribute"}," it. The owner keeps control and makes a profit, and provides support, updates and bug fixes."]],"examples":[["An artist and programmer sell an image editor as proprietary software to ",{"answer":"earn money"}," from each licence."]],"nonExamples":[["Proprietary does not mean better quality. Users buy a licence to use the software; they do not own it and have no right to view or edit the ",{"answer":"source code"},"."]]},"wordBank":["earn money","not available","redistribute","source code"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Software licence","windows":{"definition":[["A ",{"answer":"legal agreement"}," between the owner of the software and the user that sets out how the software may be used, copied, modified and distributed."]],"characteristics":[["Sets limits such as how many users or devices may run it and whether the code may be changed. ",{"answer":"Copyright"}," law backs it up, so breaking the terms is illegal."]],"examples":[["A school buys a 30-user licence for a ",{"answer":"proprietary"}," program; installing it on a 31st computer breaks the licence."]],"nonExamples":[["A licence does not transfer ownership of the software. Even ",{"answer":"open source"}," software has a licence that states what users may do with the code."]]},"wordBank":["Copyright","legal agreement","open source","proprietary"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Privacy issues","windows":{"definition":[["Concerns about how much ",{"answer":"personal information"}," about people is collected, tracked, stored and shared by technology, and who can see it."]],"characteristics":[["Points to deploy: ",{"answer":"surveillance"}," such as CCTV and facial recognition; location tracking; monitoring of posts; people feel watched; stored data could be leaked or sold."]],"examples":[["A shopping centre uses facial recognition to ",{"answer":"track"}," shoppers' movements: safer, but people feel watched."]],"nonExamples":[["Privacy is not the same as security: data can be stored securely and still be collected without people knowing or giving ",{"answer":"consent"},"."]]},"wordBank":["consent","personal information","surveillance","track"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Environmental issues","windows":{"definition":[["The effects that making, using and disposing of technology have on the ",{"answer":"natural world"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Negative: mining rare metals, ",{"answer":"energy use"}," by devices and data centres, e-waste. Positive: less travel through remote working, less paper, smart systems that save energy."]],"examples":[["A data centre runs thousands of servers all day, using huge amounts of ",{"answer":"electricity"}," for power and cooling."]],"nonExamples":[["Do not just write 'it is bad for the environment': name the impact (energy, ",{"answer":"raw materials"},", waste) and link it to the scenario to gain marks."]]},"wordBank":["electricity","energy use","natural world","raw materials"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":26,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":27,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"E-waste","windows":{"definition":[["Electronic devices, such as old phones, tablets and computers, that have been ",{"answer":"thrown away"}," because they are broken or no longer wanted."]],"characteristics":[["Often ends up in ",{"answer":"landfill"},", sometimes shipped abroad. Contains toxic chemicals that pollute soil and water, and wastes precious metals that could be recovered."]],"examples":[["People upgrade a phone that still works; the old one is binned instead of being ",{"answer":"recycled"}," or reused."]],"nonExamples":[["A phone that is repaired, donated or sold on is not e-waste. Electricity used by a device is an ",{"answer":"energy"}," issue, not e-waste."]]},"wordBank":["energy","landfill","recycled","thrown away"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":29,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":30,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Ethical issues","windows":{"definition":[["Questions about whether a use of technology is morally ",{"answer":"right or wrong"},", and whether it treats people fairly."]],"characteristics":[["Points to deploy: ",{"answer":"job losses"}," from automation; biased AI decisions; who is responsible when software fails; devices designed for short lifespans; workers' conditions."]],"examples":[["A company stops updates for older tablets so customers must buy new ones: more ",{"answer":"profit"},", but is it fair?"]],"nonExamples":[["Ethical is not the same as legal: an action can be ",{"answer":"legal"}," but still unethical, such as designing devices to need replacing after two years."]]},"wordBank":["job losses","legal","profit","right or wrong"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":32,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":33,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Cultural issues","windows":{"definition":[["The ways technology changes how groups of people live, ",{"answer":"communicate"},", work and spend their free time."]],"characteristics":[["Points to deploy: social media replaces face to face contact; ",{"answer":"remote working"},"; online shopping and streaming; pressure to own the latest device; always being contactable."]],"examples":[["Teenagers feel social pressure to own the newest smartphone to ",{"answer":"fit in"},", even though their phone still works."]],"nonExamples":[["Not the same as ethical issues: cultural points describe how behaviour and ",{"answer":"society"}," change, not whether something is right or wrong."]]},"wordBank":["communicate","fit in","remote working","society"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":35,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":36,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Digital divide","windows":{"definition":[["The gap between people who have ",{"answer":"access"}," to technology and the internet, and the skills to use it, and those who do not."]],"characteristics":[["Caused by ",{"answer":"cost"},", poor rural broadband, age, disability or lack of skills. People left out miss out on jobs, education, cheaper deals and online services."]],"examples":[["A student with no laptop or ",{"answer":"broadband"}," at home cannot complete online homework that classmates can do."]],"nonExamples":[["Someone who has a device and internet but chooses not to use social media is not ",{"answer":"excluded"},"; the divide is about being unable to get online or lacking skills."]]},"wordBank":["access","broadband","cost","excluded"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":38,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":39,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Stakeholder","windows":{"definition":[["A person, group or organisation that is ",{"answer":"affected by"},", or has an interest in, a technology or decision."]],"characteristics":[["Exam scenarios name them, such as the owners and the customers. Give ",{"answer":"benefits and drawbacks"}," for each one; the same decision can help one and harm another."]],"examples":[["Facial recognition CCTV: stakeholders are the owners, shoppers, staff and the ",{"answer":"police"},", who use footage to find suspects."]],"nonExamples":[["The technology itself is not a stakeholder. Writing only about the ",{"answer":"company"}," that makes a product, when the question also names its customers, limits the mark band."]]},"wordBank":["affected by","benefits and drawbacks","company","police"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":41,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":42,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/2.1/frayer-1.pdf","sourceSha256":"9b09f2c354a7369c816bcc680fdba1120911b0b6ba5d52ec205b3980ac943c19","code":"2.1.1","title":"Computational thinking","terms":[{"term":"Abstraction","windows":{"definition":[["Removing or hiding ",{"answer":"unnecessary detail"}," from a problem so that the important parts can be focused on."]],"characteristics":[["Reduces complexity so the problem is easier to solve. What is kept depends on the ",{"answer":"purpose"},". In exam answers, say what is kept and what is ignored in the scenario."]],"examples":[["A car star rating uses only age and ",{"answer":"miles driven"},"; make, model and colour are ignored."]],"nonExamples":[["Breaking a system into modules for security and payments is ",{"answer":"decomposition"},". A generic definition gains no marks when the question asks for an example."]]},"wordBank":["decomposition","miles driven","purpose","unnecessary detail"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Decomposition","windows":{"definition":[["Breaking a complex problem down into ",{"answer":"smaller parts"}," that are more manageable and easier to solve."]],"characteristics":[["Each part can be solved and tested separately, often as a ",{"answer":"subroutine"},", by different programmers, then combined into the full solution."]],"examples":[["A sports day program is split into ",{"answer":"events"},", each with its own subroutine, plus separate validation routines."]],"nonExamples":[["Ignoring students' favourite subjects is ",{"answer":"abstraction"},". Splitting a record into fields such as StudentID and YearGroup is not credited as decomposition."]]},"wordBank":["abstraction","events","smaller parts","subroutine"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Algorithmic thinking","windows":{"definition":[["Identifying the main steps needed to solve a problem and the ",{"answer":"sequence"}," in which the steps are completed."]],"characteristics":[["Produces a step by step solution using sequence, ",{"answer":"selection"}," and iteration, written as a flowchart or pseudocode that a person or computer can follow."]],"examples":[["Designing the step by step process for a user to ",{"answer":"pay online"}," securely: enter card, check it, take payment."]],"nonExamples":[["Stating the goal 'work out the winner' is not algorithmic thinking because no ",{"answer":"steps"}," are given. Splitting the task into modules is decomposition."]]},"wordBank":["pay online","selection","sequence","steps"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Computational thinking","windows":{"definition":[["Analysing a problem so that it can be solved in a ",{"answer":"logical way"},", in a form that a computer could carry out."]],"characteristics":[["J277 names three techniques: abstraction, ",{"answer":"decomposition"}," and algorithmic thinking. They are used together when designing a solution, before any code is written."]],"examples":[["Designing a security system: ignore unneeded data, split it into modules, then ",{"answer":"design the steps"}," for each one."]],"nonExamples":[["It is not 'thinking like a computer' and it is not ",{"answer":"programming"},": the techniques are used to plan a solution before it is coded in any language."]]},"wordBank":["decomposition","design the steps","logical way","programming"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/2.1/frayer-2.pdf","sourceSha256":"19c36dfb1dfed1a8fb4fd8bda86a9e0a9df278a47ad2db8d8ec22e1c9a2b41c2","code":"2.1.2","title":"Designing, creating and refining algorithms","terms":[{"term":"Trace table","windows":{"definition":[["A table that records how the value of each ",{"answer":"variable"}," changes, and any output, as an algorithm is followed line by line."]],"characteristics":[["One column per variable plus an ",{"answer":"output"}," column. Complete it one line at a time, writing output exactly as it is printed. Used to find logic errors."]],"examples":[["Tracing a loop that adds i to total for i = 0 to 2: total becomes 0, 1, then ",{"answer":"3"},", and the final total is printed."]],"nonExamples":[["Writing only the final answer is not a trace: every change must be shown in ",{"answer":"order"},". Leave the output column empty unless a print statement runs."]]},"wordBank":["3","order","output","variable"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Flowchart","windows":{"definition":[["A diagram that represents an algorithm using standard ",{"answer":"symbols"}," joined by arrows that show the flow of control."]],"characteristics":[["Terminal (rounded) for start/stop, parallelogram for input/output, rectangle for process, ",{"answer":"diamond"}," for a decision with yes/no branches, and a sub program box."]],"examples":[["Input age; the decision 'age >= 18?' leads to output 'adult' on ",{"answer":"yes"}," and output 'child' on no."]],"nonExamples":[["A decision written inside a rectangle is wrong: a decision needs two ",{"answer":"labelled branches"},". A structure diagram shows the parts of a problem, not the order of steps."]]},"wordBank":["diamond","labelled branches","symbols","yes"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Algorithm","windows":{"definition":[["A ",{"answer":"step by step"}," set of instructions that solves a problem or completes a task."]],"characteristics":[["Instructions are precise and in a set order; built from sequence, selection and ",{"answer":"iteration"},". Can be shown as a flowchart, pseudocode or program code."]],"examples":[["Input a width and a height, ",{"answer":"multiply"}," them to find the area, then output the area: 7 and 4 give 28."]],"nonExamples":[["A goal such as 'find the area' is not an algorithm because it gives no ",{"answer":"steps"},". An algorithm is not tied to one programming language."]]},"wordBank":["iteration","multiply","step by step","steps"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Pseudocode","windows":{"definition":[["A text-based way of designing an algorithm using structured, English-like statements, without the strict ",{"answer":"syntax"}," of a programming language."]],"characteristics":[["Cannot be run by a computer. There is no single correct form, but it must be clear and logical, with ",{"answer":"indentation"}," showing the code inside loops and selection."]],"examples":[["IF stock = 0 THEN OUTPUT \\"Sold out\\" describes ",{"answer":"selection"}," without being in any particular language."]],"nonExamples":[["Vague prose such as 'deal with the stock' is not pseudocode. The more formal language used in exam papers is ",{"answer":"OCR Exam Reference Language"},"."]]},"wordBank":["indentation","OCR Exam Reference Language","selection","syntax"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Input","windows":{"definition":[["Data that is ",{"answer":"entered into"}," an algorithm or program, for example typed by the user or read from a sensor or a file."]],"characteristics":[["Shown in a ",{"answer":"parallelogram"}," in a flowchart. In OCR Exam Reference Language: name = input(\\"Enter name\\"). The value is normally stored in a variable."]],"examples":[["height = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\") stores the value the user ",{"answer":"types"}," in the variable height."]],"nonExamples":[["Setting score = 0 in the code is an ",{"answer":"assignment"},", not an input, because no data comes from outside the program. print() is output."]]},"wordBank":["assignment","entered into","parallelogram","types"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Output","windows":{"definition":[["Information that a program ",{"answer":"displays"}," or sends out to the user or to another device once data has been processed."]],"characteristics":[["Shown in a parallelogram in a flowchart. In OCR Exam Reference Language use ",{"answer":"print()"},". In a trace table it goes in the output column exactly as it would appear."]],"examples":[["If score holds 3, print(score * 2) displays ",{"answer":"6"}," on screen; the value stored in score does not change."]],"nonExamples":[["Changing a variable, such as score = score * 2, is a ",{"answer":"process"},"; nothing is output until a print statement runs."]]},"wordBank":["6","displays","print()","process"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Process","windows":{"definition":[["An action carried out on data by an algorithm, such as a ",{"answer":"calculation"},", a comparison or changing the value of a variable."]],"characteristics":[["Shown in a ",{"answer":"rectangle"}," in a flowchart. Turns the inputs into the outputs. Includes arithmetic, assignment, sorting and searching."]],"examples":[["Ticket prices 4, 6 and 5 are input; total = 4 + 6 + 5 is the process and ",{"answer":"15"}," is output."]],"nonExamples":[["Asking the user for a price is input, and displaying the total is ",{"answer":"output"},"; only the working out in between is the process."]]},"wordBank":["15","calculation","output","rectangle"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Structure diagram","windows":{"definition":[["A diagram that shows how a problem is ",{"answer":"decomposed"}," into smaller sub-problems, arranged in levels below the overall task."]],"characteristics":[["The overall problem is at the ",{"answer":"top"},"; each level below breaks a task into smaller tasks. The lowest boxes are small enough to code as a single subroutine."]],"examples":[["A parents' evening app splits into log in, book, view and update; View splits into ",{"answer":"on screen"}," or print."]],"nonExamples":[["It is not a ",{"answer":"flowchart"},": the lines show which tasks belong to which, not the order of steps or the result of decisions."]]},"wordBank":["decomposed","flowchart","on screen","top"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":26,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":27,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Syntax error","windows":{"definition":[["An error where the code breaks the ",{"answer":"rules"}," of the programming language, so it cannot be translated or run."]],"characteristics":[["Found by the translator, which reports the ",{"answer":"line"},". The program will not run until it is fixed. Causes: misspelt keywords, missing brackets or quotes, missing endif."]],"examples":[["prnt(\\"Hello\\") has a misspelt ",{"answer":"keyword"},", so the program will not run until it is corrected to print."]],"nonExamples":[["A program that runs but gives the wrong answer, such as using + instead of *, has a ",{"answer":"logic error"},". A syntax error stops the program from running at all."]]},"wordBank":["keyword","line","logic error","rules"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":29,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":30,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Logic error","windows":{"definition":[["An error in the design of an algorithm: the program runs without crashing but produces an ",{"answer":"unexpected result"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Not reported by the translator, so it is found by ",{"answer":"testing"}," or a trace table. Causes: wrong operator such as < instead of <=, wrong loop limits, steps in the wrong order."]],"examples":[["A loop meant to read five scores uses for i = 1 to 4, so the program runs but the ",{"answer":"total"}," is wrong."]],"nonExamples":[["A missing bracket or misspelt keyword is a ",{"answer":"syntax error"},": the program will not run at all. With a logic error there is no error message."]]},"wordBank":["syntax error","testing","total","unexpected result"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":32,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":33,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"OCR Exam Reference Language","windows":{"definition":[["The formal language OCR uses to present ",{"answer":"algorithms"}," in J277 exam papers, with set keywords for input, output, selection, loops and subroutines."]],"characteristics":[["Uses print() and input(), == for comparison, MOD and DIV, and closing keywords such as endif, ",{"answer":"next i"},", endwhile and endfunction. Arrays are indexed from 0."]],"examples":[["for i = 0 to 4 ... next i is a count-controlled loop that repeats ",{"answer":"5"}," times: i goes from 0 to 4 inclusive."]],"nonExamples":[["It cannot be run on a computer, and you are not forced to write in it: answers may use a ",{"answer":"high-level language"}," such as Python instead."]]},"wordBank":["5","algorithms","high-level language","next i"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":35,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":36,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/2.1/frayer-3.pdf","sourceSha256":"8089dff685a3f054efe0f8f38e33d54e3f3569a3f851b8eed9a59778b69eb608","code":"2.1.3","title":"Searching and sorting algorithms","terms":[{"term":"Binary search","windows":{"definition":[["A search that compares the target with the ",{"answer":"middle"}," item of a sorted list and discards the half that cannot contain it, repeating until found."]],"characteristics":[["Pre-requisite: the data must already be ",{"answer":"sorted"},". If the target is smaller keep the left half; if larger keep the right half. More efficient than a linear search on large lists."]],"examples":[["Find 10 in 1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 10, 20: middle is 6; 10 is ",{"answer":"bigger"},", so discard 1 to 6; the new middle is 10: found."]],"nonExamples":[["Do not write only 'split the list in half': the middle item must be ",{"answer":"compared"}," first. Not found means no items are left to check, not that the end of the list was reached."]]},"wordBank":["bigger","compared","middle","sorted"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Linear search","windows":{"definition":[["A search that checks each item ",{"answer":"in order"}," from the start of the list until the target is found or the end of the list is reached."]],"characteristics":[["Works on ",{"answer":"unsorted"}," data and is simple to code. Stops when the item is found or when every item has been checked. Slow for large lists."]],"examples":[["Search 12, 5, 9, 3 for 9: compare 12, then 5, then 9: found at index ",{"answer":"2"}," after three comparisons."]],"nonExamples":[["Jumping to the middle item and discarding half is a ",{"answer":"binary search"},". 'Checks each value' alone is not enough: say it checks from the first item to the last."]]},"wordBank":["2","binary search","in order","unsorted"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Merge sort","windows":{"definition":[["A sort that ",{"answer":"splits"}," the list repeatedly until each sub-list holds one item, then merges the sub-lists back together in order."]],"characteristics":[["A ",{"answer":"divide and conquer"}," algorithm. When merging two sorted lists, compare the front items and take the smaller each time. Fast on large lists but uses more memory."]],"examples":[["5, 2, 8, 1 splits into 5 | 2 | 8 | 1, merges into 2, 5 and 1, 8, then merges into ",{"answer":"1, 2, 5, 8"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Joining two lists and then sorting them is not a merge and gains no marks: items must be ",{"answer":"compared"}," as the lists are combined. Bubble sort never splits the list."]]},"wordBank":["1, 2, 5, 8","compared","divide and conquer","splits"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Insertion sort","windows":{"definition":[["A sort that takes each item in turn and inserts it into its correct place in a ",{"answer":"sorted section"}," at the start of the list."]],"characteristics":[["The list has a sorted part and an ",{"answer":"unsorted"}," part. Larger items are moved up one place to make room. Efficient for small or nearly sorted lists; sorts in place."]],"examples":[["Sorted part 2, 7 and next item 4: 7 moves right and 4 is inserted, giving ",{"answer":"2, 4, 7"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Comparing neighbouring pairs and swapping them across a whole pass is a ",{"answer":"bubble sort"},". Insertion sort compares each new item only with the items to its left."]]},"wordBank":["2, 4, 7","bubble sort","sorted section","unsorted"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Bubble sort","windows":{"definition":[["A sort that compares each pair of ",{"answer":"adjacent"}," items and swaps them if they are in the wrong order, repeating passes until the list is sorted."]],"characteristics":[["After each pass the largest unsorted item is in its final place. It stops when a full pass makes ",{"answer":"no swaps"},". Simple to code but slow on large lists."]],"examples":[["One pass on 4, 1, 3: swap 4 and 1, then swap 4 and 3, giving ",{"answer":"1, 3, 4"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Splitting the list into single items and recombining them is a ",{"answer":"merge sort"},". One pass is not always enough: passes repeat until no swaps are made."]]},"wordBank":["1, 3, 4","adjacent","merge sort","no swaps"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/2.2/frayer-1.pdf","sourceSha256":"d25a631a747d719515efdaaea91bec764e3b9ef98a8aa881457b99ab2826af3d","code":"2.2.1","title":"Programming fundamentals","terms":[{"term":"Selection","windows":{"definition":[["Selection is a programming construct where a ",{"answer":"condition"}," decides which path of code is run next."]],"characteristics":[["Uses if, ",{"answer":"elseif"},", else and endif, or switch and case. Each branch runs at most once; it is never repeated."]],"examples":[["if temp < 10 then print(\\"Cold\\") else print(\\"Warm\\") endif. When temp is 7 the output is ",{"answer":"Cold"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A while loop also makes a decision, but it repeats its code, so it is ",{"answer":"iteration"},", not selection. An if statement never loops back."]]},"wordBank":["Cold","condition","elseif","iteration"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Iteration","windows":{"definition":[["Iteration is a programming construct where a section of code is ",{"answer":"repeated"},", either a set number of times or until a condition is met."]],"characteristics":[["Two types: count-controlled (for ... next) and ",{"answer":"condition-controlled"}," (while ... endwhile, do ... until). It avoids writing the same lines of code many times."]],"examples":[["do pin = input(\\"PIN\\") until pin == \\"2580\\". A wrong PIN makes the code ",{"answer":"loop back"}," and ask again."]],"nonExamples":[["An if statement checks a condition but runs its code only once, so it is ",{"answer":"selection"},". Iteration must go round and run the same code again."]]},"wordBank":["condition-controlled","loop back","repeated","selection"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Count-controlled iteration (FOR)","windows":{"definition":[["Count-controlled iteration repeats a block of code a ",{"answer":"fixed number"}," of times, using a counter variable."]],"characteristics":[["Written as for i = 0 to 4 ... next i. The counter goes up by 1 each pass (or by a ",{"answer":"step"}," value). Used when the number of repeats is known before the loop starts."]],"examples":[["total = 0, then for i = 1 to 3 total = total + i next i. After the loop, total is ",{"answer":"6"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Asking for a password until it is correct needs a condition-controlled loop such as ",{"answer":"while"},": the number of attempts is not known in advance."]]},"wordBank":["6","fixed number","step","while"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Condition-controlled iteration (WHILE, DO UNTIL)","windows":{"definition":[["Condition-controlled iteration repeats a block of code while, or until, a ",{"answer":"condition"}," is met."]],"characteristics":[["while ... endwhile tests at the start, so it may run ",{"answer":"zero times"},". do ... until tests at the end, so it always runs at least once."]],"examples":[["x = 10 and y = 0. Each pass of while x > 0 runs y = y + 1 then x = x - y. The loop stops after ",{"answer":"4"}," passes."]],"nonExamples":[["for i = 0 to 9 is not condition-controlled: it always repeats 10 times. A while loop whose test never becomes False is an ",{"answer":"infinite loop"},"."]]},"wordBank":["4","condition","infinite loop","zero times"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Sequence","windows":{"definition":[["Sequence is a programming construct where instructions are executed one after another, in the ",{"answer":"order"}," they are written."]],"characteristics":[["Every line runs ",{"answer":"once"},", from top to bottom, with no decisions and no loops. Changing the order of the lines can change the result."]],"examples":[["value = 3, value = value - 1, print(value * 2). Run in this order, the output is ",{"answer":"4"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Order matters: print(value * 2) placed before value = value - 1 would output ",{"answer":"6"},". Choosing between paths is selection; repeating code is iteration."]]},"wordBank":["4","6","once","order"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Variable","windows":{"definition":[["A variable is a named memory location that stores a value which ",{"answer":"can change"}," while the program is running."]],"characteristics":[["It has an ",{"answer":"identifier"}," (name) and a data type. Assigning a new value overwrites the old one. Meaningful names make the code easier to maintain."]],"examples":[["score = 4 then score = score + 3. The variable score now stores ",{"answer":"7"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A value that must stay fixed, such as pi = 3.142, should be a ",{"answer":"constant"},". The number 4 on its own is a value, not a variable."]]},"wordBank":["7","can change","constant","identifier"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Constant","windows":{"definition":[["A constant is a named memory location storing a value that ",{"answer":"cannot change"}," while the program is running."]],"characteristics":[["Declared with const in OCR reference language. Its value is ",{"answer":"set once"},", when it is declared; editing that one line updates it everywhere it is used."]],"examples":[["const pi = 3.142 then area = pi * r ^ 2. With r = 10 the area is ",{"answer":"314.2"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A score that goes up during a game must be a ",{"answer":"variable"},". Many languages report an error if the program changes a constant; Python does not, so the program must never reassign one."]]},"wordBank":["314.2","cannot change","set once","variable"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Assignment","windows":{"definition":[["Assignment ",{"answer":"stores a value"}," in a variable or constant, using the = operator."]],"characteristics":[["The value or expression on the ",{"answer":"right"}," is worked out first, then stored in the identifier on the left. Any previous value is overwritten."]],"examples":[["count = 5 then count = count + 1. The second line increments count, so it now holds ",{"answer":"6"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["== is not assignment: it is a ",{"answer":"comparison"}," operator. if x == 5 tests the value of x, but x = 5 changes it."]]},"wordBank":["6","comparison","right","stores a value"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":26,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":27,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"MOD","windows":{"definition":[["MOD is an arithmetic operator that gives the ",{"answer":"remainder"}," after one number is divided by another."]],"characteristics":[["Written as a MOD b. If x MOD 2 == 0 then x is ",{"answer":"even"},". The result is always smaller than the number you divide by."]],"examples":[["17 MOD 5 = ",{"answer":"2"},", because 5 fits into 17 three times, making 15, and the rest is left over."]],"nonExamples":[["17 MOD 5 is not 3: the whole number of times 5 goes into 17 is given by ",{"answer":"DIV"},". MOD has nothing to do with percentages."]]},"wordBank":["2","DIV","even","remainder"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":29,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":30,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"DIV","windows":{"definition":[["DIV is the arithmetic operator for ",{"answer":"integer division"},": it gives the whole number part of a division and discards the remainder."]],"characteristics":[["Written as a DIV b. The answer is always an ",{"answer":"integer"},"; the remainder is discarded, never rounded up. Often used together with MOD."]],"examples":[["135 DIV 60 = ",{"answer":"2"},", so 135 minutes is 2 whole hours. 135 MOD 60 = 15 gives the minutes left over."]],"nonExamples":[["19 DIV 5 is not 3.8 and not 4. Normal division ( / ) gives 3.8; DIV gives ",{"answer":"3"}," because the decimal part is cut off, not rounded."]]},"wordBank":["2","3","integer","integer division"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":32,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":33,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Arithmetic operator","windows":{"definition":[["An arithmetic operator is a symbol that performs a ",{"answer":"calculation"}," on numbers, such as add or multiply."]],"characteristics":[["+ add, - subtract, * multiply, / divide, MOD remainder, DIV integer division, ^ ",{"answer":"exponentiation"}," (to the power of). Brackets are worked out first."]],"examples":[["2 ^ 3 = ",{"answer":"8"},", 7 * 2 = 14, 9 / 2 = 4.5, 9 DIV 2 = 4 and 9 MOD 2 = 1."]],"nonExamples":[["==, > and <= are ",{"answer":"comparison"}," operators: they give True or False, not a number. AND, OR and NOT are Boolean operators."]]},"wordBank":["8","calculation","comparison","exponentiation"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":35,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":36,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Comparison operator","windows":{"definition":[["A comparison operator compares two values and gives a ",{"answer":"Boolean"}," result: True or False."]],"characteristics":[["== equal to, != ",{"answer":"not equal to"},", < less than, <= less than or equal to, > greater than, >= greater than or equal to. Used in conditions for if and while."]],"examples":[["With age = 15: age >= 15 is True, age == 16 is ",{"answer":"False"}," and age != 16 is True."]],"nonExamples":[["A single = is ",{"answer":"assignment"},", not comparison. Write >= not =>. The operators + and DIV are arithmetic because they calculate a number."]]},"wordBank":["assignment","Boolean","False","not equal to"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":38,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":39,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Boolean operator","windows":{"definition":[["A Boolean operator (AND, OR, NOT) ",{"answer":"combines or reverses"}," conditions to give one True or False result."]],"characteristics":[["AND: True only if ",{"answer":"both"}," conditions are True. OR: True if at least one condition is True. NOT: changes True to False and False to True."]],"examples":[["if armed AND (door OR window) then. With armed True, door False, window True the condition is ",{"answer":"True"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["if pin != \\"1234\\" OR \\"4321\\" is wrong. Each side must be a ",{"answer":"full comparison"},": pin != \\"1234\\" AND pin != \\"4321\\"."]]},"wordBank":["both","combines or reverses","full comparison","True"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":41,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":42,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/2.2/frayer-2.pdf","sourceSha256":"793e57eeb58c1a5c13cc5964397befee09898b5b414267520d7219bca34e61a6","code":"2.2.2","title":"Data types","terms":[{"term":"Casting","windows":{"definition":[["Casting is converting a value from one ",{"answer":"data type"}," to another."]],"characteristics":[["Uses int(), str() and float(). Needed because input() returns a string, which must be cast to a number before doing ",{"answer":"arithmetic"},"."]],"examples":[["num = int(\\"1234\\") casts the string to an integer, so print(num * 2) outputs ",{"answer":"2468"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["int(\\"four\\") does not give 4: text that is not a valid number causes an ",{"answer":"error"},". Casting does not check or validate the data."]]},"wordBank":["2468","arithmetic","data type","error"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"String","windows":{"definition":[["A string is a data type that stores a sequence of ",{"answer":"characters"},": letters, digits, symbols and spaces."]],"characteristics":[["Written inside ",{"answer":"quotation marks"},". Used for names, postcodes and phone numbers. Strings can be concatenated and measured with .length."]],"examples":[["teamName = \\"Red Kites\\" is a string. A phone number like \\"07700 900123\\" is a string so the ",{"answer":"leading zero"}," is kept."]],"nonExamples":[["\\"12\\" + \\"3\\" gives \\"123\\", not 15: digits stored as strings are ",{"answer":"joined"},", not added. Cast to an integer first to do arithmetic."]]},"wordBank":["characters","joined","leading zero","quotation marks"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Integer","windows":{"definition":[["An integer is a data type that stores a ",{"answer":"whole number"},", which can be positive, negative or zero."]],"characteristics":[["Has no ",{"answer":"fractional"}," part. Used for counting and for array indexes, e.g. number of nights, year group. Can be used in arithmetic, unlike a string."]],"examples":[["yearGroup = 11 and nights = 3 are integers. 7 DIV 2 also gives an integer: ",{"answer":"3"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A javelin throw of 14.3 m has a decimal part, so it needs ",{"answer":"real"}," (float). A phone number should be a string, not an integer."]]},"wordBank":["3","fractional","real","whole number"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Real","windows":{"definition":[["A real is a data type that stores a number with a ",{"answer":"fractional part"}," (a decimal part)."]],"characteristics":[["Also called ",{"answer":"float"},". Used for measurements, prices and averages. Dividing two integers with / can give a real, e.g. 9 / 2 = 4.5."]],"examples":[["javelinThrow = 14.3 and price = 2.50 are real. The average of 7 and 8 is ",{"answer":"7.5"},", which is also real."]],"nonExamples":[["The number of students in a team is always a whole number, so use ",{"answer":"integer"},". \\"14.3\\" in quotation marks is a string, not a real."]]},"wordBank":["7.5","float","fractional part","integer"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Boolean","windows":{"definition":[["A Boolean is a data type that can store only one of ",{"answer":"two values"},": True or False."]],"characteristics":[["The result of any ",{"answer":"comparison"}," is Boolean, e.g. score >= 5. Used for yes/no data and flags, e.g. stayComplete, systemArmed, and to control if and while."]],"examples":[["score = 8 then passed = score >= 5 stores ",{"answer":"True"}," in passed. A function can also return a Boolean."]],"nonExamples":[["\\"True\\" in quotation marks is a ",{"answer":"string"},", not a Boolean. A field with three options, e.g. Basic, Premium or Deluxe, cannot be Boolean."]]},"wordBank":["comparison","string","True","two values"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Character","windows":{"definition":[["A character is a data type that stores a ",{"answer":"single"}," letter, digit or symbol."]],"characteristics":[["Written in quotation marks, e.g. \\"A\\". Each character is stored as a binary code from a ",{"answer":"character set"}," such as ASCII. Also called char."]],"examples":[["grade = \\"B\\", a menu choice \\"Y\\" and the symbol \\"?\\" are each one character. ASC(\\"A\\") returns ",{"answer":"65"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["\\"Cat\\" is ",{"answer":"three"}," characters, so it must be a string. The digit \\"7\\" stored as a character is not the integer 7: it must be cast before arithmetic."]]},"wordBank":["65","character set","single","three"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Data type","windows":{"definition":[["A data type defines the ",{"answer":"kind of data"}," a variable can store and the operations that can be carried out on it."]],"characteristics":[["J277 uses ",{"answer":"five"}," data types. The right choice saves memory and allows the right operations, e.g. arithmetic on numbers."]],"examples":[["teamName: string. yearGroup: integer. distance: real. stayComplete: ",{"answer":"Boolean"},". grade: character."]],"nonExamples":[["Storing a price as a string means it cannot be used in ",{"answer":"calculations"}," until it is cast. An array or a record is a data structure, not a data type."]]},"wordBank":["Boolean","calculations","five","kind of data"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/2.2/frayer-3.pdf","sourceSha256":"7921e2abf4b0c76f0eda8aa574cdf079d4a144f6832fbf5778b8a702f189e7f1","code":"2.2.3","title":"Additional programming techniques","terms":[{"term":"Sub program","windows":{"definition":[["A sub program is a named, self-contained block of code that performs a ",{"answer":"specific task"}," and can be called from elsewhere in the program."]],"characteristics":[["Two types: functions and ",{"answer":"procedures"},". Reusable, so the program is shorter and easier to test, debug and maintain."]],"examples":[["A procedure showMenu() is written ",{"answer":"once"}," and called each time the menu is needed, instead of copying the print lines."]],"nonExamples":[["Pasting the same 10 lines in five places is not a sub program: it is ",{"answer":"duplicated code"},", and any fix must be made five times."]]},"wordBank":["duplicated code","once","procedures","specific task"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Function","windows":{"definition":[["A function is a sub program that ",{"answer":"returns a value"}," to the part of the program that called it."]],"characteristics":[["Starts with function and ends with endfunction. Can take ",{"answer":"parameters"},". The call is used in an expression or assignment, e.g. total = calculatePrice(3)."]],"examples":[["function calculatePrice(t) return t * 60 endfunction. The call calculatePrice(4) returns ",{"answer":"240"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A sub program that only prints a message and returns nothing is a ",{"answer":"procedure"},". Printing a result inside a function is not the same as returning it."]]},"wordBank":["240","parameters","procedure","returns a value"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Procedure","windows":{"definition":[["A procedure is a sub program that carries out a task but does ",{"answer":"not return"}," a value."]],"characteristics":[["Starts with procedure and ends with endprocedure. Can take parameters. It is called on its ",{"answer":"own line"},", e.g. greet(\\"Ali\\"), not in an expression."]],"examples":[["procedure greet(name) print(\\"Hello \\" + name) endprocedure. The call greet(\\"Ali\\") outputs ",{"answer":"Hello Ali"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["x = greet(\\"Ali\\") is wrong: a procedure gives nothing back to store. If the caller needs a result, use a ",{"answer":"function"}," with return."]]},"wordBank":["function","Hello Ali","not return","own line"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Parameter","windows":{"definition":[["A parameter is a value that is ",{"answer":"passed into"}," a sub program when it is called, listed in brackets after the sub program's name."]],"characteristics":[["Acts like a ",{"answer":"local variable"}," inside the sub program. There can be several, separated by commas. Parameters let one sub program work on different data."]],"examples":[["function newPrice(nights, room) has two parameters. newPrice(5, \\"premium\\") passes in 5 and ",{"answer":"\\"premium\\""},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A value fixed inside the sub program, e.g. nights = 5, is not a parameter. The ",{"answer":"return value"}," comes out of a function; parameters go in."]]},"wordBank":["\\"premium\\"","local variable","passed into","return value"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,