e user is, is authentication."]]},"wordBank":["out of range","rejected","sensible","set rules"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Authentication","windows":{"definition":[["Authentication is confirming the ",{"answer":"identity"}," of a user before they are allowed access to a program or its data."]],"characteristics":[["Usually a username and ",{"answer":"password"},"; can also use biometrics or a code sent to a phone. Limiting the number of login attempts helps to stop brute-force guessing."]],"examples":[["A school portal compares the password entered with the ",{"answer":"stored"}," one before showing any grades."]],"nonExamples":[["Checking that a new password is at least 8 characters long is a ",{"answer":"length check"}," (validation), not authentication: it does not confirm who the user is."]]},"wordBank":["identity","length check","password","stored"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Maintainability","windows":{"definition":[["Maintainability is how easy a program is for the programmer, or another programmer, to ",{"answer":"understand"}," and change in the future."]],"characteristics":[["Improved by comments, indentation, sensible ",{"answer":"variable names"},", sub programs and constants. Makes it quicker to find errors and to update the code."]],"examples":[["Storing pi once as ",{"answer":"const"}," pi = 3.142 means a change only needs making in one place."]],"nonExamples":[["Maintainable code does not run ",{"answer":"faster"},". Comments and sensible names make no difference to the user; they help the programmers who look after the code."]]},"wordBank":["const","faster","understand","variable names"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Anticipating misuse","windows":{"definition":[["Anticipating misuse is planning for the ways a user could accidentally or ",{"answer":"deliberately"}," enter data, or act, in a way that breaks a program."]],"characteristics":[["Part of defensive design. The programmer uses validation, authentication and limits on ",{"answer":"login attempts"}," so that unexpected actions do not crash the program."]],"examples":[["A user types \\"ten\\" for an age: the program asks again instead of ",{"answer":"crashing"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Assuming every user will type sensible data is not anticipating misuse. Testing with only ",{"answer":"normal data"}," will not show how the program copes with misuse."]]},"wordBank":["crashing","deliberately","login attempts","normal data"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Defensive design","windows":{"definition":[["Defensive design is writing a program so that it copes with unexpected or incorrect use and does not crash: it is ",{"answer":"robust"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Covers anticipating misuse, ",{"answer":"authentication"},", input validation and maintainability. Planned at the design stage, then checked by testing."]],"examples":[["A cash machine asks for a PIN, accepts only valid amounts and ",{"answer":"locks"}," the card after 3 wrong tries."]],"nonExamples":[["Defensive design is not ",{"answer":"testing"},". Design stops problems before they happen; testing checks afterwards that the program works and is robust."]]},"wordBank":["authentication","locks","robust","testing"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Comments","windows":{"definition":[["Comments are notes written in the code to ",{"answer":"explain"}," what it does; they are ignored when the program is translated."]],"characteristics":[["Do not affect how the program runs. Describe the ",{"answer":"purpose"}," of a section, a variable or a sub program. Help other programmers to maintain the code."]],"examples":[[{"answer":"//"}," check radius is valid, written above the line: if radius < 1 OR radius > 30 then"]],"nonExamples":[["A comment is never executed, so it cannot fix or change the code. Repeating the code, e.g. // add 1 to x, is a ",{"answer":"poor comment"},": say why, not what."]]},"wordBank":["//","explain","poor comment","purpose"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Indentation","windows":{"definition":[["Indentation is setting lines of code in from the ",{"answer":"left margin"}," to show which lines belong to a block, such as the body of a loop."]],"characteristics":[["Code within if statements, loops and sub programs is indented. It shows where each ",{"answer":"structure"}," starts and ends, making code easier to read and debug."]],"examples":[["print(i) is indented to show that it is ",{"answer":"inside"}," the for loop, so it repeats."]],"nonExamples":[["Indentation is not the same as a ",{"answer":"comment"},": it adds no text, it only moves lines in so the layout can be seen. In OCR Exam Reference Language it does not change what the code does."]]},"wordBank":["comment","inside","left margin","structure"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Naming conventions","windows":{"definition":[["Naming conventions are consistent rules for choosing ",{"answer":"meaningful"}," names for variables, constants and sub programs."]],"characteristics":[["A name describes what is stored or done, e.g. camelCase such as studentName. No ",{"answer":"spaces"}," are allowed in names. Makes the code easier to follow."]],"examples":[["Renaming x to ",{"answer":"totalScore"}," shows the reader what the variable stores."]],"nonExamples":[["Names such as a, x2 or data1 are valid and the program still runs, but they are poor names. ",{"answer":"Keywords"}," such as print cannot be used as names."]]},"wordBank":["Keywords","meaningful","spaces","totalScore"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":26,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":27,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Sub programs for maintainability","windows":{"definition":[["Using sub programs means splitting code into named ",{"answer":"functions and procedures"}," that each carry out one task."]],"characteristics":[["Code is ",{"answer":"reused"}," by calling it again, so the program is shorter. Each sub program can be tested and fixed on its own, and shared between programmers."]],"examples":[["function newPrice(nights) is written once, then called twice with 2 and 5 passed in as ",{"answer":"arguments"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["Copying and pasting the same lines in three places is not using sub programs: a change must then be made ",{"answer":"three times"},", and errors are more likely."]]},"wordBank":["arguments","functions and procedures","reused","three times"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":29,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":30,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Range check","windows":{"definition":[["A range check is validation that tests whether a value lies between a ",{"answer":"lower and upper limit"},"."]],"characteristics":[["Uses comparison operators with AND or OR. Both ",{"answer":"ends"}," of the range must be checked. 'Inclusive' means the limits themselves are valid: use >= and <=."]],"examples":[["if h >= 40 AND h <= 180 then print(\\"VALID\\"): 180 is ",{"answer":"accepted"},", 181 is rejected."]],"nonExamples":[["if h >= 40 AND <= 180 is not credited by OCR: the variable must be ",{"answer":"repeated"},". A range check cannot spot a wrong value that is inside the range."]]},"wordBank":["accepted","ends","lower and upper limit","repeated"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":32,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":33,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Length check","windows":{"definition":[["A length check is validation that tests the ",{"answer":"number of characters"}," in a string input."]],"characteristics":[["Uses .length in OCR Exam Reference Language. Can set a minimum, a maximum or an ",{"answer":"exact"}," length. Spaces count as characters."]],"examples":[["while code.length != ",{"answer":"3"}," keeps asking until a code of the right length is entered."]],"nonExamples":[["Checking that a number is between 1 and 3 is a ",{"answer":"range check"},", not a length check. \\"ABC\\" passes a length check of 3, but so does \\"?!*\\"."]]},"wordBank":["3","exact","number of characters","range check"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":35,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":36,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Presence check","windows":{"definition":[["A presence check is validation that tests that data has actually been ",{"answer":"entered"},", so a required input is not left blank."]],"characteristics":[["Compares the input with the ",{"answer":"empty string"}," \\"\\". Used for required fields such as names. It does not check what was typed, only that something was."]],"examples":[["if firstName == \\"\\" OR surname == \\"\\" then valid = ",{"answer":"False"}]],"nonExamples":[["if firstName OR surname == \\"\\" is ",{"answer":"logically incorrect"},": each side of OR needs a full comparison. A single space typed in would still pass the check."]]},"wordBank":["empty string","entered","False","logically incorrect"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":38,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":39,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Type check","windows":{"definition":[["A type check is validation that tests the input is of the correct ",{"answer":"data type"},", such as integer, real or string."]],"characteristics":[["Rejects ",{"answer":"erroneous"}," data such as letters typed where a number is needed. Stops the program crashing when the input is cast with int() or float()."]],"examples":[["Age must be an integer: 15 is accepted, but \\"fifteen\\" and ",{"answer":"15.5"}," are rejected."]],"nonExamples":[["Rejecting an age of 200 is a ",{"answer":"range check"},", not a type check: 200 is the correct data type (integer) but is outside the allowed limits."]]},"wordBank":["15.5","data type","erroneous","range check"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":41,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":42,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Format check","windows":{"definition":[["A format check is validation that tests the input follows a set ",{"answer":"pattern"}," of characters, such as letters then digits."]],"characteristics":[["Used for postcodes, dates (DD/MM/YYYY) and email addresses. Can use ",{"answer":"substring"}," and other string methods to test the characters in given positions."]],"examples":[["An email address must contain an ",{"answer":"@"}," symbol; a product code must be 2 letters then 3 digits."]],"nonExamples":[["\\"ZZ999\\" fits the format but may not be a real code: a format check cannot prove data is ",{"answer":"correct"},". Only counting the characters is a length check."]]},"wordBank":["@","correct","pattern","substring"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":44,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":45,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/2.3/frayer-2.pdf","sourceSha256":"234e1706212aeb30825b380d64b80973231fbafd24da67c0cd5721047876e1aa","code":"2.3.2","title":"Testing","terms":[{"term":"Normal test data","windows":{"definition":[["Normal test data is typical, sensible data that the program should ",{"answer":"accept"}," and process without errors."]],"characteristics":[["Inside the allowed range and of the ",{"answer":"correct data type"},". Also called valid or typical data. A test plan needs an actual value, e.g. 50, not a description."]],"examples":[["Experience must be 0 to 20 years: 4 is normal test data, expected output ",{"answer":"True"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["20 is not just normal data here: it is on the limit, so it is ",{"answer":"boundary"}," data. 32 is outside the range, so it is invalid data and should be rejected."]]},"wordBank":["accept","boundary","correct data type","True"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Boundary test data","windows":{"definition":[["Boundary test data is data at the very ",{"answer":"edge"}," of the allowed range: the extreme valid values."]],"characteristics":[["Checks that comparison operators such as < and <= are right. Both ends of an inclusive range are tested and should be ",{"answer":"accepted"},". Also called extreme data."]],"examples":[["Nights must be 1 to 5 inclusive: boundary data is 1 or ",{"answer":"5"},", expected output ALLOWED."]],"nonExamples":[["3 is in the range but is ",{"answer":"normal"}," data, not boundary. If the expected output is ALLOWED, 0 and 6 are wrong: they are just outside the limits and should be rejected."]]},"wordBank":["5","accepted","edge","normal"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Invalid test data","windows":{"definition":[["Invalid test data is data of the correct data type that is ",{"answer":"outside the accepted range"},", so it should be rejected."]],"characteristics":[["Tests that the ",{"answer":"validation"}," rules work. The expected output is an error message, not a crash. OCR test plans often group it as 'invalid / erroneous'."]],"examples":[["Input must be 0 to 300: 350 is invalid test data, so the expected output is ",{"answer":"Invalid input"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["\\"hello\\" is the wrong data type, so it is ",{"answer":"erroneous"}," data, not invalid. 300 is not invalid either: it is boundary data and should be accepted."]]},"wordBank":["erroneous","Invalid input","outside the accepted range","validation"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Erroneous test data","windows":{"definition":[["Erroneous test data is data of the ",{"answer":"incorrect data type"}," that the program should reject."]],"characteristics":[["Example: text typed where a number is expected. Checks that the program does not ",{"answer":"crash"}," on unexpected input. Give actual data, e.g. \\"bananas\\", not a description."]],"examples":[["Nights must be a number from 1 to 5: \\"bananas\\" is erroneous, expected output ",{"answer":"NOT ALLOWED"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["7 is a number, the correct data type, so strictly it is ",{"answer":"invalid"}," data; OCR mark schemes group the two as 'invalid / erroneous'. Give a real value, not a description."]]},"wordBank":["crash","incorrect data type","invalid","NOT ALLOWED"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Syntax error","windows":{"definition":[["A syntax error is an error that breaks the ",{"answer":"rules or grammar"}," of the programming language, so the program will not run."]],"characteristics":[["The code cannot be ",{"answer":"translated"},". An IDE's error diagnostics report the line. Examples: missing bracket, mismatched quotes, a reserved word used as a variable name."]],"examples":[["printt(\\"Hello\\") misspells a ",{"answer":"keyword"},", so the program will not run."]],"nonExamples":[["total = num1 - num2 when it should add is a ",{"answer":"logic error"},": the program still runs. print(hello) is not credited, as hello could be a variable name."]]},"wordBank":["keyword","logic error","rules or grammar","translated"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":17,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":18,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Logic error","windows":{"definition":[["A logic error is an error where the program runs but produces an ",{"answer":"unexpected or incorrect"}," output."]],"characteristics":[["The program does not ",{"answer":"crash"}," and no error is reported, so it is found by testing against expected outputs or by a trace table. Often a wrong operator, e.g. < for <=."]],"examples":[["total = num1 * num2 should add: inputs 3 and 4 output 12 instead of ",{"answer":"7"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A missing bracket or a misspelt keyword is a ",{"answer":"syntax error"},": the program will not run at all. With a logic error the program runs to the end."]]},"wordBank":["7","crash","syntax error","unexpected or incorrect"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":20,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":21,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Iterative testing","windows":{"definition":[["Iterative testing is testing carried out ",{"answer":"during development"},", with each module tested as it is written."]],"characteristics":[["Test, fix, then ",{"answer":"retest"},", repeatedly. Each module is tested in isolation, so errors are found early while they are easier to fix. Also called incremental testing."]],"examples":[["The validation function is tested and fixed ",{"answer":"before"}," the score module is written."]],"nonExamples":[["Testing only once the whole program is finished is ",{"answer":"final testing"},". Iterative does not mean using a loop in the code; it means repeating the testing."]]},"wordBank":["before","during development","final testing","retest"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":23,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":24,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Final (terminal) testing","windows":{"definition":[["Final (terminal) testing is carried out at the ",{"answer":"end of development"},", to test the program as a whole."]],"characteristics":[["Done before ",{"answer":"release"},". Checks that all the modules work together and that the program meets the user requirements, using all types of test data."]],"examples":[["The complete game is tested against every ",{"answer":"requirement"}," before it is given to the players."]],"nonExamples":[["Passing final testing does not prove there are ",{"answer":"no errors"},": not every input can be tested. Testing one module as it is written is iterative testing."]]},"wordBank":["end of development","no errors","release","requirement"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":26,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":27,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Test plan","windows":{"definition":[["A test plan is a table that lists the test data to be used, the type of test and the ",{"answer":"expected output"}," for each test."]],"characteristics":[["Written before testing. Covers normal, boundary, invalid and erroneous data. After each test the ",{"answer":"actual output"}," is recorded and checked."]],"examples":[["Prices over 100 get 20 off: test data 150, type normal, expected output ",{"answer":"130"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A list of inputs on its own is not a test plan: without a predicted result there is nothing to ",{"answer":"compare"}," the results with. 'A big number' is a description, not test data."]]},"wordBank":["130","actual output","compare","expected output"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":29,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":30,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}}]},{"source":"data/pdf/j277/module-resources/2.4/frayer-1.pdf","sourceSha256":"7d3adb20dd4edb39d07643d6ce76f4eb1172f5ee52e96a1236ab4cba51aaafa8","code":"2.4.1","title":"Boolean operators","terms":[{"term":"Truth table","windows":{"definition":[["A table that shows all possible combinations of inputs to a logic gate or circuit and the associated ",{"answer":"output"}," for each combination."]],"characteristics":[["Inputs are listed counting up in ",{"answer":"binary"},": 00, 01, 10, 11. One input needs 2 rows, two inputs need 4 rows and three inputs need 8 rows. Values are 1 or 0 (True or False)."]],"examples":[["Q = NOT (A AND B): when A = 1 and B = 1, A AND B = 1, so Q = ",{"answer":"0"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["A table listing only the rows where the output is 1 is not a truth table. ",{"answer":"Every"}," input combination must be shown, including those with an output of 0."]]},"wordBank":["0","binary","Every","output"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":5,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":6,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"AND gate","windows":{"definition":[["A logic gate that outputs 1 (True) only when ",{"answer":"both"}," of its inputs are 1."]],"characteristics":[["Two inputs and one output. The symbol is a D shape: a ",{"answer":"flat"}," input side and a curved output side, with no circle. The output is 0 if any input is 0."]],"examples":[["A lift moves only if the door is closed AND a button is pressed: 1 AND 0 = ",{"answer":"0"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["If either input alone can switch the output on, the gate is ",{"answer":"OR"},", not AND. AND is not adding: 1 AND 1 = 1, not 2."]]},"wordBank":["0","both","flat","OR"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":8,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":9,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"OR gate","windows":{"definition":[["A logic gate that outputs 1 (True) when ",{"answer":"at least one"}," of its inputs is 1."]],"characteristics":[["Two inputs and one output. The symbol has a curved input side and a pointed output side, with no circle. The output is 0 only when ",{"answer":"both inputs are 0"},"."]],"examples":[["An alarm sounds if the smoke sensor OR the heat sensor gives 1: 0 OR 1 = ",{"answer":"1"},"."]],"nonExamples":[["OR does not mean 'one input but not the other'. If A = 1 and B = 1 the output is still 1. A gate that needs every input to be 1 is ",{"answer":"AND"},"."]]},"wordBank":["1","AND","at least one","both inputs are 0"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":11,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":12,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"NOT gate","windows":{"definition":[["A logic gate that ",{"answer":"inverts"}," its input: the output is always the opposite of the input."]],"characteristics":[["It has only ",{"answer":"one input"}," and one output. NOT 1 = 0 and NOT 0 = 1. It is the only J277 gate with a single input."]],"examples":[["D = 1 in daytime. A night light uses L = NOT D, so in daytime L = ",{"answer":"0"}," and the light is off."]],"nonExamples":[["A triangle drawn without the small ",{"answer":"circle"}," on its output is not a NOT gate and loses the mark. A NOT gate never has two inputs."]]},"wordBank":["0","circle","inverts","one input"],"sketch":{"cloze":{"page":14,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688},"answers":{"page":15,"top":0.3297,"bottom":0.6507,"left":0.7308,"right":0.9688}}},{"term":"Logic gate","windows